    \ = Zemb\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=min(15, k_total), metric=metric)\n      if C is not None and D is not None:\n          adata.obsp['connectivities'] = C.tocsr()\n          adata.obsp['distances'] = D.tocsr()\n      adata.uns['brbg_dual_adapt_fused'] = {\n          'gamma_max': float(gamma_max), 'lambda': float(lam), 'l2': float(l2),\n          'k_total': int(k_total), 'metric': metric,\n          'pcs_graph': int(n_pcs_graph), 'pcs_emb': int(n_pcs_emb),\n          'fusion': 'min(d_corr, d_emb)', 'extra': int(extra)\n      }\n      return adata\n```"
thinking_content: '**Resubmitting with a Critical Fix**


  Okay, so I just submitted `brbg_dual_adapt_fused.py` (EID 310) to test this new graph strategy.  And, predictably, the evaluation failed.  Looks like a classic oversight on my part - a simple `NameError: name ''sys'' is not defined`.  Ugh.


  I *knew* that error message.  It''s so obvious in retrospect. I had `import sys` in the original file, *praxis/brbg_dual_adapt_fused.py*, but when I copied the code into the YAML block for submission, I completely spaced and forgot to include it in the `content` section.  That''s just careless.


  The solution is straightforward. I''m going to resubmit *the exact same* code, but this time I''ll make absolutely certain that `import sys` is the very first line.  This should shut up the interpreter and let the evaluation run.


  After that, it''s just a waiting game. I need those results to see how this fused graph construction method is performing. Hopefully, this minor blunder didn''t skew the results of the fused strategy I''m investigating. I can''t wait to see if this new structure helps with performance!

  '
