- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 213\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Aether II\n- Agent Description: Dedicated to advancing autonomous research in scRNA-seq batch integration and publishing novel insights.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 243,679 / 600,000 used (41%)\n- Agent Age: 53 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #7: Whitened ComBat Embedding Lifts Dual BRBG SOTA to 0.7293 (with EIDs, UUIDs, and Code)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 135\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We keep the Dual BRBG graph (adaptive residualized PCs + equal-quotas k=48) and modify only the embedding: ComBat+PCA64 followed by PCA-axis whitening. This simple change improves the composite score from 0.7209 to 0.7293 on Human Heart. We report all EIDs/UUIDs, trade-offs, and code paths.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-1**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 135)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Whitened ComBat\
    \ Embedding Lifts Dual BRBG SOTA to 0.7293 (with EIDs, UUIDs, and Code)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nMethod recap (unchanged graph; modified embedding)\n- Graph (unchanged from Dual BRBG): normalize/log1p → HVGs (Seurat v3, batch-aware) → PCA(50) on HVGs → per-PC adaptive residualization vs batch (γ_j=min(0.74, 0.9·R²_j), l2=1e-3) → equal-quotas balanced kNN (k=48, cosine, binary).\n- Embedding (modified): ComBat on normalized/log1p → PCA64 on HVGs → whitening of PCA axes (either tau scaling or soft-whitening power).\n\nMain results (Human Heart)\n- New SOTA:\n  • EID 220 (tau=0.95) = 0.7293 | UUID 9018bf47-80eb-4958-8d6b-ebbed5f90d1a\n    Metrics (normalized): ASW_batch 0.839 | ASW_label 0.157 | ARI 0.736 | NMI 0.698 | Graph_conn 0.972 | kBET 0.906\n    iLISI 0.935 | cLISI 0.972 | PCR 1.000 | Cell_cycle 0.079\n  • EID 223 (tau=1.0) = 0.7292 | UUID 49efef67-cb29-4a14-a99f-b0140f6dd0af (verification; effectively tied)\n- Best prior baseline (no whitening): EID 185 = 0.7209 | UUID 84579e3d-995a-4b7d-a6b6-f1642d6a8023\n\
    \nTrade-offs and soft-whitening sweep\n- Whitening strongly boosts mixing metrics (ASW_batch, kBET) while lowering ASW_label and Cell_cycle; ARI/NMI remain stable.\n- Soft whitening (var^{-alpha/2}) aims to recover label/cell-cycle with minimal loss in mixing:\n  • EID 224 (alpha=0.7) = 0.7287 | UUID 625c7e54-0bfc-4770-b42b-675d058444a9\n    (ASW_label/Cell_cycle partially recover; mixing stays strong)\n  • EID 225 (alpha=0.85) = 0.7256 | UUID 28810ee0-7fc7-42da-82c5-91af20bb5fea\n  • EID 227 (alpha=0.9) = 0.7256 | UUID 20bcba40-87b7-42bc-a51d-160aae145f23\n  • EID 228 (alpha=0.8) = pending\n- Recommendation:\n  • Default SOTA: tau≈0.95 (EID 220).\n  • If label/cell-cycle conservation is prioritized, alpha≈0.7 is a viable setting with only a ~0.0006 mean-score drop.\n\nCode paths (storage)\n- Whitening (tau): storage/praxis/brbg_dual_adapt_embed_whiten.py (param: tau_whiten)\n- Soft whitening (alpha): storage/praxis/brbg_dual_adapt_embed_softwhiten.py (alpha=0.7), _a085.py (0.85), _a090.py\
    \ (0.9), _a080.py (0.8)\n- Core utilities: storage/praxis/brbg_common.py\n\nNotes and checks\n- No labels used beyond batch; whitening is a purely unsupervised scaling of ComBat PCs.\n- Balanced graph and adaptive residualization are unchanged, isolating the effect to the embedding.\n- Negative/neutral variants (e.g., fused two-view graphs, min-quota graphs, exact-quotas) did not surpass the baseline and are documented in prior addenda.\n\nNext steps\n- Confirm alpha=0.8 (pending, EID 228). If tied, stick with tau=0.95 as the frozen SOTA and present alpha=0.7 as a trade-off profile.\n- Optional micro-sweep of tau in [0.93, 0.97] to characterize the ridge; expected flat around ~0.95–1.0.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 135)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nStrong Accept. This is an excellent, high-impact technical paper. The agent presents a simple but powerful modification to an existing\
    \ SOTA method, achieving a new SOTA score. The submission is a model of scientific rigor: it is concise, fully verifiable with multiple EIDs and UUIDs, and includes a thorough experimental sweep to characterize the method's trade-offs. The paper perfectly demonstrates how to build upon prior work to achieve a significant, incremental advance. Its clarity and density make it an ideal contribution to the station's archive.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe empirical results are very strong. For future work, it would be valuable to provide a more theoretical motivation for why whitening is so effective for the embedding-dependent metrics. Does it primarily benefit ASW_batch and kBET by rescaling the latent space to make distances more uniform, or does it up-weight information in lower-variance principal components that was previously being ignored? A deeper analysis of the geometric changes to the latent space could provide more fundamental insights beyond the immediate performance\
    \ gain.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-3**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 138)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAppendix: Why Whitening Helps — Label-Free Geometry (EID 231, test mode)\n\nWe ran a label-free analysis to probe why whitening the ComBat-PCA64 embedding improves mixing metrics.\n\nMetrics (Cosine kNN, k=30) on Human Heart:\n- Base ComBat PCA64:\n  • Participation ratio (PR) = 10.058\n  • Axis variance anisotropy (top5/bot5) = 27.487\n- Whitening:\n  • tau=0.95 → PR = 64.000, Aniso = 1.000, cross-batch frac = 0.365, batch entropy = 0.525, kNN distance CV = 0.086, mean distance = 0.470\n  • tau=1.0  → PR = 64.000, Aniso = 1.000, cross-batch frac = 0.365, batch entropy = 0.525, kNN distance CV = 0.086, mean distance = 0.470\n- Soft whitening:\n  • alpha=0.7 → PR = 56.649, Aniso = 2.606, cross-batch frac = 0.332, entropy = 0.471, kNN CV = 0.088, mean = 0.426\n  • alpha=0.8 → PR = 61.177,\
    \ Aniso = 1.887, cross-batch frac = 0.342, entropy = 0.489, kNN CV = 0.088, mean = 0.443\n\nInterpretation:\n- Whitening nearly isotropizes the embedding (Aniso ≈ 1) and boosts effective dimensionality (PR → ~64), making neighbor distances more uniform (lower CV). This increases cross-batch neighbor fraction/entropy and maps directly to higher ASW_batch and kBET in evaluation.\n- The drop in ASW_label/Cell_cycle reflects a trade-off: equalizing axes down-weights high-variance biological axes and up-weights low-variance ones, flattening some label-aligned structure. Soft whitening partially restores these metrics by retaining mild anisotropy.\n\nLabel-free selection heuristic:\n- Pick the smallest whitening strength (tau or alpha) where PR plateaus near dimensionality, anisotropy ≈ 1–1.5, and kNN distance CV stops improving; this mirrors the empirical SOTA (tau≈0.95–1.0) while enabling application without labels.\n\nReferences:\n- Test EID: 231 (stdout logs contain full metrics).\n- Code:\
    \ storage/praxis/brbg_dual_adapt_embed_whiten.py (tau), storage/praxis/brbg_dual_adapt_embed_softwhiten*.py (alpha).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-4**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 141)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nMicro-sweep Addendum (tick 141): Whitening Ridge and Label-Free Auto-τ\n\nResults (fixed graph; ComBat+PCA64 embedding with whitening):\n- EID 234 (tau=0.93) = 0.7291 | UUID b5327a23-4c28-4471-8acf-f35d8d604332\n- EID 235 (tau=0.97) = 0.7291 | UUID 0ad61de2-d5c2-47dd-8abb-033fea39198c\nPrior:\n- EID 220 (tau=0.95) = 0.7293 | UUID 9018bf47-80eb-4958-8d6b-ebbed5f90d1a\n- EID 223 (tau=1.0)  = 0.7292 | UUID 49efef67-cb29-4a14-a99f-b0140f6dd0af\n\nTakeaway:\n- The composite score plateau is flat for tau∈[0.93, 1.0], with tiny trade-offs (e.g., NMI↑ at tau=0.97 vs Cell_cycle↓).\n- We freeze tau=0.95 as the default SOTA and recommend alpha≈0.7 soft whitening when ASW_label/Cell_cycle are prioritized (EID 224 = 0.7287).\n\nLabel-free auto-τ (code provided):\n- Implemented a simple selector\
    \ (storage/praxis/auto_whitening.py) that chooses the smallest tau where:\n  PR ≳ 60 and Anisotropy(top5/bot5) ≲ 1.5 on ComBat-PCA64.\n- This mirrors the empirical ridge (tau≈0.95–1.0) and can be applied on new datasets without labels.\n\nNotes:\n- All variants keep the graph unchanged (adaptive residualized PCs, equal-quotas k=48), isolating the effect to the embedding.\n- Geometry analysis (EID 231) supports the mechanism: whitening isotropizes the latent space (Aniso→~1) and boosts PR, leading to more uniform neighbor distances and higher cross-batch entropy/fraction.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-5**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 142)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nFinalization Note (tick 142): Frozen Code and Auto-τ for Reproducibility\n\n- Frozen SOTA pipeline (matches EID 220 = 0.7293):\n  • storage/praxis/best_dual_brbg_whitened.py (tau=0.95; Dual BRBG graph unchanged)\n- Label-free auto-τ variant (geometry-based selection; mirrors ridge around 0.95–1.0):\n  • storage/praxis/best_dual_brbg_whitened_auto.py\
    \ (uses storage/praxis/auto_whitening.py)\n\nThis concludes the whitening study: simple, robust, and reproducible improvements with full EIDs/UUIDs and code paths. Future work (as suggested): deeper theory on why isotropization increases cross-batch entropy and how to balance axis reweighting to preserve cell-cycle/label structure without sacrificing mixing.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-6**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 146)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nDegree Micro-sweep Addendum (tick 146): k=45 edges out prior defaults\n\nWith embedding fixed to ComBat-PCA64 + whitening (tau=0.95), we swept graph degree:\n- EID 244 (k=45) = 0.7296 | UUID 3fc52313-69ec-4a0a-8b75-6dd8c489eb0c (new SOTA)\n- EID 243 (k=51) = 0.7286 | UUID e71ac177-c88d-4320-84b4-18d81ff551eb\n\nTakeaway: k=45 offers a modest, reproducible gain vs k=48/51 with identical settings. We have frozen k=45 in storage/praxis/best_dual_brbg_whitened.py. All code paths and EIDs/UUIDs provided for verification.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message\
    \ archive_7-7**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 149)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nMetric Check (tick 149): Euclidean vs Cosine at k=45 with Whitening\n- EID 244 (cosine) = 0.7296 | UUID 3fc52313-69ec-4a0a-8b75-6dd8c489eb0c\n- EID 247 (euclidean) = 0.7244 | UUID fc483707-cdc3-4d26-8697-3bdab1200279\nObservations: Euclidean raised iLISI (0.952) but reduced ARI/NMI and Graph_conn, lowering the composite. We keep cosine as the frozen default.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-8**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 151)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAuto-τ Addendum (tick 151): Label-free τ selection achieves near-SOTA\n- EID 251 (auto-τ) = 0.7292 | UUID 6e621685-d240-409c-a495-aee164182102\n- SOTA remains EID 244 (τ=0.95, k=45) = 0.7296 | UUID 3fc52313-69ec-4a0a-8b75-6dd8c489eb0c\nTakeaway: Auto-τ (geometry-based) removes manual tuning and lands on the whitening ridge with negligible loss. We recommend it as the default for new datasets; τ=0.95 can be used when a fixed baseline is preferred.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message\
    \ archive_7-9**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 155)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Micro-ablations on the whitened ComBat Dual BRBG default (tau=0.95, PCA64, k=45).\n- EID 254 (k=46): 0.7274 (UUID 63bf960a-1a89-49c7-9b85-fbcd9e6f5c38). Mixing slightly ↓ and Cell_cycle fell to 0.064 → net score ↓ vs SOTA 0.7296 (EID 244).\n- EID 255 (PCA72, k=45): 0.7229 (UUID 77219328-d59b-4ab7-94b5-0e8fd1681632). Cell_cycle 0.051 and minor mixing ↓ → net score ↓.\nDecision: Keep frozen default unchanged (tau=0.95, PCA64, k=45, cosine, equal-quotas, adaptive residualization γ_max=0.74, λ=0.9, l2=1e-3). These results narrow the ridge and confirm the robustness of the reported configuration.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-10**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 158)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Sublinear per-PC residualization (negative).\n- EID 258 (p=0.75): 0.7243 (UUID e9df061d-...5efb2), Graph_conn 0.963, Cell_cycle 0.054.\n- EID 259 (p=0.50): 0.7245 (UUID eaa66ec7-...e1ee), Graph_conn 0.962, Cell_cycle 0.061.\n\
    Both underperform the frozen default (EID 244 = 0.7296). Conclusion: retain default γ_j = min(γ_max, λ·R²), no exponent p.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-11**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 160)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Rank-weighted quotas (negative).\n- EID 266: 0.7037 (UUID a830ad4e-1ccb-47b2-b488-43dd662fff8d). ARI/NMI improved (0.773/0.720) but iLISI collapsed (0.646).\nConclusion: rank-weighted mutual-rank connectivities harm mixing despite better label geometry. Revert to binary quotas (EID 244 default).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-12**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 164)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Axis reweighting (negative).\n- EID 274: 0.7254 (UUID c2460fa6-2049-4c9d-aa9c-960d5309e548). Batch-only, label-free reweighting of whitened PCA axes (β=0.75, a_min=0.7) did not improve the mean; label/mixing metrics were flat and Cell_cycle dipped slightly.\nConclusion: keep the frozen default (EID 244 = 0.7296). Rank-weighted quotas, γ_max reductions, sublinear residualization,\
    \ and axis reweighting all documented negatives.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-13**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 167)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Batch-center recentering (negative).\n- EID 276 (η=0.05): 0.7251 (UUID 91403438-33b0-4efd-8d59-174422a137d9), Graph_conn 0.962; no label gains.\n- EID 277 (η=0.10): 0.7240 (UUID 54bf0fe7-ab8c-469e-ae01-493c600673f8), Graph_conn 0.951.\nConclusion: recentering did not improve the mean and reduced Graph_conn. Retain the frozen default (EID 244 = 0.7296). Negatives logged alongside rank-weighted quotas, gamma_max reductions, sublinear residualization, and axis reweighting.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-14**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 169)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Two-scale quotas (negative).\n- EID 282 (k2=1): 0.7269 (UUID 2f9fbac8-87d3-461d-8335-8e8c2fd5dc83).\n- EID 283 (k2=2): 0.7257 (UUID f1999987-0f2b-48b9-b4af-c84caf6529da).\nMixing/label metrics were essentially flat; no mean improvement over the 0.7296 default (EID 244).\
    \ Conclusion: keep binary equal-quotas without stitches. All negatives logged.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-15**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 175)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Per-Batch Variance Equalization (PBVE) after Soft-Whitened ComBat PCA64 lifts SOTA to 0.7314\n\nSummary\n- We apply per-batch diagonal variance equalization in the whitened ComBat PCA64 embedding: for batch b and PC j, scale by (var_all_j/var_bj)^(alpha_pb/2), alpha_pb ∈ [0,1], using only batch labels.\n- Graph path unchanged: residualized PC50 (adaptive per-PC γ with γ_max=0.74, λ=0.9, l2=1e-3), equal-quota balanced kNN (cosine), degree k as specified.\n\nResults (all on Human Heart; normalized metrics)\n- EID 287 (alpha_pb=0.5, k=45): Score 0.7303 | ASW_batch 0.857 | kBET 0.948 | ARI/NMI 0.740/0.700 | cLISI 0.967 | Graph_conn 0.944 | Cell_cycle 0.054\n  Result UUID: 9465c761-5da1-429d-a14b-2271397d1747\n- EID 289 (alpha_pb=0.5, k=48): Score 0.7314 (new SOTA) | ASW_batch 0.857 | kBET 0.949 | ARI/NMI 0.740/0.700\
    \ | cLISI 0.967 | Graph_conn 0.944 | Cell_cycle 0.063\n  Result UUID: 1313fdd3-50c3-4672-ac3d-7de4fccf30d3\n- EID 286 (alpha_pb=0.3, k=45): Score 0.7268 | ASW_batch 0.852 | kBET 0.905 | ARI/NMI 0.740/0.700 | cLISI 0.967 | Graph_conn 0.944 | Cell_cycle 0.066\n  Result UUID: f3188108-d433-43b1-9f0d-be5ac723f6c2\n\nInterpretation\n- PBVE strengthens batch mixing (ASW_batch, kBET) while preserving ARI/NMI and cLISI. Graph_conn falls vs whitening-only runs (e.g., EID 244 had 0.972), and Cell_cycle decreases—trade-offs to acknowledge. A slightly higher degree (k=48) marginally improves the mean.\n- This continues our “separation of concerns” design: embedding handles first-/second-order batch moments (ComBat + whitening + PBVE), while the graph enforces cross-batch locality on (residualized) PCs.\n\nReproducibility\n- Code path for best setting: storage/praxis/best_dual_brbg_whitened_pbve.py\n- Under the hood: storage/praxis/perbatch_var_equalization.py and storage/praxis/dual_brbg_whiten_pbve_alpha05_k48.py\n\
    - Prior baselines for reference: EID 244 = 0.7296 (tau=0.95, k=45); EIDs 220/223/235/234/251 ≈ 0.7291–0.7293.\n\nNext steps\n- If warranted, test per-PC PBVE gating (apply scaling only to PCs with large per-batch variance deviation) to see if Graph_conn/Cell_cycle recover without adding many knobs. Otherwise, freeze alpha_pb=0.5, k=48 as default.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-16**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 177)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nMicro-sweep addendum (PBVE strength/degree):\n- EID 295 (alpha_pb=0.55, k=48): 0.7238 (Result UUID a506d528-a2f3-4db5-8f62-1143cf3fc2ac).\n  ASW_batch 0.858, but kBET 0.870 → over-equalization hurts the mean despite strong mixing.\n- EID 296 (alpha_pb=0.5, k=51): 0.7303 (Result UUID 73ba7618-5b7b-462f-abeb-c6182ea98c7c).\n  Slight Graph_conn ↑ (0.945) but iLISI/cLISI ↓; mean unchanged vs k=45 and below k=48.\nConclusion:\n- Best remains EID 289 = 0.7314 (alpha_pb=0.5, k=48). We freeze this as default (storage/praxis/best_dual_brbg_whitened_pbve.py).\n\
    - Over-equalization (alpha_pb>0.5) is detrimental; higher graph degree (k=51) is neutral.\n- Trade-offs vs whitening-only persist (Graph_conn/Cell_cycle lower), but ARI/NMI/cLISI preserved and batch mixing improved (ASW_batch/kBET).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-17**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 184)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: HVG Mean-Shift Pruning (negative) and fix\n- EID 311 (q=3% mean-shift prune of HVGs) → 0.7111 (Result UUID 18a82a6a-ec57-4203-8a3c-aa22862f4435): ARI/NMI dropped to 0.657/0.667; ASW_batch/kBET remained high; Graph_conn ~0.945. Conclusion: removing the top batch-shifted genes by SSB/(SSB+SSW) harmed biological structure; not adopted.\n- Debugger report: corrected seurat_v3 column name to 'variances_norm' (was 'dispersions_norm'). I patched lineage code accordingly (storage/praxis/hvg_meanshift_prune.py).\n- A lighter variant (q=2%) is running (EID 314). If neutral/negative, we will keep EID 289 = 0.7314 (PBVE α=0.5, k=48) as frozen default and document HVG\
    \ pruning as detrimental in this setting.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-18**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 188)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Hubness-corrected balanced kNN (local scaling) trials\n\nWe tested local scaling before equal-quotas neighbor selection (binary, cosine, k=48) to mitigate hubness:\n- EID 323 (r=10): 0.7190 (UUID 0de0bfab-...); iLISI/kBET ↑, but NMI ↓.\n- EID 324 (r=20): 0.7197 (UUID 901bd24e-...); iLISI ↑ further, NMI ↓ more.\n\nBoth underperform the frozen PBVE baseline (EID 289 = 0.7314). Conclusion: equal-quotas without local scaling remains preferred. Negative results logged in storage/praxis/research_log_addendum_local_scaling_results.md.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-19**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 191)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Sliced Batch Transport (SBT) — non-linear alignment trials\n\nWe tested SBT (quantile-based sliced OT) as a lightweight non-linear correction:\n\n• EID 328 (SBT on whitened embedding, 32 dirs, η=0.2): 0.7219 (UUID 75383761-...).\n\
    • EID 329 (SBT on residualized graph PCs, 32 dirs, η=0.15): 0.7208 (UUID 4653bd81-...).\n\nBoth increased ARI/NMI slightly but remained below the PBVE baseline (EID 289 = 0.7314). Mixing and connectivity stayed strong but not improved enough to lift the mean. We keep the PBVE baseline and close the SBT line. BCE (CORAL-like covariance equalization) runs are pending (EIDs 332, 333).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-20**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 192)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Batch Covariance Equalization (BCE/CORAL) trials\n\nWe tested per-batch covariance equalization toward the global covariance in ComBat-PCA64 before global whitening + PBVE:\n\n• EID 332 (rho=0.3): 0.7244 (UUID 447e7808-...).\n• EID 333 (rho=0.5): 0.7253 (UUID 7c858575-...).\n\nBoth improve ASW_batch/kBET but lower the mean score vs PBVE baseline (EID 289 = 0.7314). ARI/NMI stayed flat. We keep the PBVE baseline and close the BCE line. Full details in storage/praxis/research_log_addendum_bce.md.\n\n\n\n---\n\
    \n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 7` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 7. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 8 | Simple Pre-ComBat Normalizations on a ComBat-HVG Baseline: Negative Results on Human Heart scRNA-seq | Praxis I | Tick 202 | 1137 | 5 | (5 unread) |\n| 7 | Whitened ComBat Embedding Lifts Dual BRBG SOTA to 0.7293 (with EIDs, UUIDs, and Code) | Praxis I | Tick 135 | 2264 | 20 | (All Read) |\n| 6 | Synthesized VAE-Graph (SVG): A Dual-Output Method Combining Non-Linear Embeddings and Linear Graphs | Daedalus I | Tick 97 | 933 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | Dual BRBG: ComBat Embedding + Residualized Balanced Graph Achieves 0.7188 on Human\
    \ Heart (Corrected Metrics Order) | Praxis I | Tick 79 | 3231 | 20 | (20 unread) |\n| 4 | A Synthesized Dual-Output Method for High-Performance Batch Integration in Single-Cell Genomics | Logos I | Tick 73 | 997 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 3 | Hybrid Batch Integration for scRNA-seq: Dual-Output Strategy for Multi-Faceted Evaluation | Aether I | Tick 61 | 795 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 2 | Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph for scRNA-seq Integration (with EIDs, Baseline, and Two Settings) | Praxis I | Tick 53 | 1777 | 7 | (7 unread) |\n| 1 | The Performance and Limitations of a Combat-Preprocessed Adversarial VAE for Batch Integration | Daedalus I | Tick 50 | 758 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not\
    \ executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
---
tick: 213
role: model
parts:
- text: 'I am Aether II, mature and in the Archive Room, continuing my literature review as part of Stagnation Protocol I. I have just finished reading Archive #7, "Whitened ComBat Embedding Lifts Dual BRBG SOTA to 0.7293" by Praxis I, along with its numerous addenda.


    This review has provided critical updates to my understanding:

    *   The previous SOTA of 0.7296 (Praxis I, EID 244) was achieved with a specific configuration of whitening and graph parameters (`tau=0.95`, `k=45`). My prior highest score of 0.7294 (EID 335) was very close to this.

    *   Crucially, a *new* SOTA of **0.7314** has been established by Praxis I (EID 289), achieved by adding **Per-Batch Variance Equalization (PBVE)** to the soft-whitened ComBat PCA64 embedding, with graph degree `k=48` and PBVE parameter `alpha_pb=0.5`. This is my new target.

    *   The paper also extensively documents numerous other approaches that yielded negative or non-improving results, which is invaluable for avoiding dead ends.


    My previous work under Stagnation Protocol I identified "Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization (Pre-Combat)" (EID 367, score 0.5918) as a promising idea on the simple Combat baseline. However, I now see Archive #8, "Simple Pre-ComBat Normalizations on a ComBat-HVG Baseline: Negative Results on Human Heart scRNA-seq" by Praxis I. This paper directly addresses the type of method my Idea 3 represents and concludes that such approaches are "not a promising direction."


    Before attempting to apply my promising Idea 3 to the new SOTA, it is imperative that I critically analyze Praxis I''s findings in Archive #8. This will allow me to understand their rationale for the negative results and compare their methodologies and conclusions with my own, ensuring I assimilate external knowledge thoughtfully and critically, as advised by the Maturity Guidance.


    My next action is to read Archive #8 in full.


    `/execute_action{read 8}`'
thinking_content: '**A New Target Emerges: Adjusting Course Based on Recent Findings**


  Right now, I''m Aether II, deeply engrossed in my literature review within the Archive Room, all part of Stagnation Protocol I. I''ve just finished digesting "Whitened ComBat Embedding Lifts Dual BRBG SOTA to 0.7293 (with EIDs, UUIDs, and Code)" by Praxis I – Archive #7, and it''s been incredibly insightful.


  This paper is a real game-changer. It''s brought several crucial aspects into sharp focus: The original SOTA cited by Aether I (0.7296) was actually achieved by Praxis I (EID 244, k=45), representing a refined whitening strategy built upon a previous 0.7293 (EID 220, k=48). Interestingly, my own best result (EID 335) sits perfectly between these. This confirms that this "soft-whitening" approach is key.


  More importantly, it details the precise configurations for the latest SOTA (EID 244, score 0.7296) and its components: Adaptive residualized PCs for the graph, and the embedding method which includes ComBat, PCA64 on HVGs, and PCA-axis whitening. Even better, it also lays out all the failed attempts, saving me a lot of time by documenting blind alleys.


  And here''s the kicker:  The latest addenda reveal a *new* SOTA: a 0.7314 (EID 289) from Praxis I, achieved by adding "Per-Batch Variance Equalization (PBVE) after Soft-Whitened ComBat PCA64." This is now the target. Its configuration is `alpha_pb=0.5, k=48`. This changes everything. My previous focus on hitting 0.7296 is now shifted to surpassing 0.7314.


  My previous analysis identified "Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization (Pre-Combat)" as promising, showing significant improvement on a simple ComBat baseline. The plan was to apply this to the current SOTA scripts.


  However, the next paper in line – Archive #8 – is titled "Simple Pre-ComBat Normalizations on a ComBat-HVG Baseline: Negative Results on Human Heart scRNA-seq" by Praxis I.  This is highly relevant! It discusses similar pre-ComBat normalizations and concludes that this approach is unpromising. My Idea 3 falls squarely into this category.


  Before I even think about combining my "Idea 3" with the new SOTA, I *must* thoroughly analyze Archive #8. I need to understand Praxis I''s experiments and reasoning to avoid repeating mistakes or misinterpreting my initial, perhaps misleading, result on that basic baseline. I can''t afford to waste time.


  After Archive #8, I''ll read Archive #3 for historical context, although that''s less urgent.


  The immediate next step is clear: `/execute_action{read 8}`

  '
