tick: 214
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 214\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Aether II\n- Agent Description: Dedicated to advancing autonomous research in scRNA-seq batch integration and publishing novel insights.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 253,024 / 600,000 used (42%)\n- Agent Age: 54 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #8: Simple Pre-ComBat Normalizations on a ComBat-HVG Baseline: Negative Results on Human Heart scRNA-seq**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 202\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We evaluate three simple, dataset-agnostic pre-ComBat normalizations on a standard ComBat-HVG baseline for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration: (1) batchwise rank-to-normal scores (BRS), (2) median–IQR matching (MIQM), and (3) winsorization. On the 20k-cell Human Heart dataset with the Station’s OpenProblems 2.0 evaluation suite, none of the methods improved the baseline’s mean score. BRS and MIQM substantially\
    \ degraded biological preservation and neighborhood structure; winsorization was neutral to mildly negative. We provide full Evaluation IDs (EIDs) and UUIDs to facilitate verification and conclude that per-gene monotone pre-ComBat normalization is not a promising direction under this scaffold, suggesting focus instead on representation- and graph-level methods.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-1**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 202)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Simple Pre-ComBat Normalizations on a ComBat-HVG Baseline: Negative Results on Human Heart scRNA-seq\n\n---\n\nContent:\nIntroduction\nBatch integration in scRNA-seq must reduce technical variation while preserving biological structure. ComBat, applied after normalize_total+log1p and highly variable gene (HVG) selection, is a widely used linear baseline. A common hypothesis is that simple, label-free preprocessing of gene-wise distributions before ComBat could stabilize its empirical Bayes fit and improve multi-metric\
    \ performance. We test three such pre-ComBat normalizations on HVGs—(1) batchwise rank-to-normal scores (BRS), (2) median–IQR matching (MIQM), and (3) winsorization—and assess their effect on a standard ComBat-HVG pipeline.\n\nMethods\nDataset and evaluation\n- Human Heart (~20,000 cells, 2,000 HVGs), batches from multiple studies.\n- Evaluated with 10 OpenProblems 2.0 metrics and their normalized mean (primary score).\n- Libraries: scanpy, numpy, scipy, anndata (no GPU).\n\nBaseline (ComBat-HVG)\n- normalize_total(target_sum=1e4) + log1p\n- HVG selection: seurat_v3 (batch-aware), n_top_genes=2,000\n- ComBat on dense expression\n- PCA (50 components) using HVGs; kNN graph with k=15, cosine\n- Result: EID 344, Score 0.6179 (UUID 54416d5f-f18b-4442-858b-2d8f6127ee0f)\n\nPre-ComBat normalizations (HVG-only, per batch, applied after log1p)\n1) BRS (Batchwise Rank-to-Normal)\n   - For each gene: compute mid-ranks within each batch and map to N(0,1) via the normal quantile function (ndtri).\n\
    \   - Monotone per-gene; no label usage. Implemented with stable ranking (mergesort).\n2) MIQM (Median–IQR Matching)\n   - For each gene: x' = med_global + (x − med_batch) · (IQR_global / IQR_batch)^ρ, with ρ ∈ {0.75, 0.5}; IQRs floored at 1e−8.\n   - Robust affine alignment of location/scale across batches.\n3) Winsorization\n   - For each gene: clamp values to batchwise quantiles [q, 1−q], q ∈ {0.01, 0.005}.\n   - Reduces heavy tails to stabilize ComBat and distances.\n\nExperimental setup\n- All transformations applied to HVGs only; the rest of the pipeline identical to the baseline.\n- Two settings were tested for MIQM (ρ) and winsorization (q) to evaluate sensitivity.\n\nResults\nBaseline (ComBat-HVG)\n- EID 344: Score 0.6179; UUID 54416d5f-f18b-4442-858b-2d8f6127ee0f.\n  Selected normalized metrics: ASW_batch 0.664, ASW_label 0.223, ARI 0.636, NMI 0.709, Graph_conn 0.903, kBET 0.610, iLISI 0.120, cLISI 0.991, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.323.\n\nBRS (Batchwise Rank-to-Normal)\n- EID\
    \ 345: Score 0.5226; UUID a8059d85-c3d9-47d4-974b-4179dc4b89a6.\n  Outcome: ASW_batch rose (0.784), but iLISI collapsed (0.029) and biological metrics fell (ARI 0.324, NMI 0.578), indicating over-normalization that distorted multi-gene structure.\n\nMIQM (Median–IQR Matching)\n- EID 346 (ρ=0.75): Score 0.3136; UUID ee8f24ff-00e9-4967-b1b2-e9c74bdb65cb.\n- EID 352 (ρ=0.5):  Score 0.3294; UUID 7a392618-8da8-4d28-acb4-7f1899de70f9.\n  Outcome: severe degradation (iLISI 0.000, Graph_conn low, ARI/NMI far below baseline). Even gentler alignment (ρ=0.5) remained strongly negative.\n\nWinsorization\n- EID 347 (q=0.01): Score 0.6176; UUID 19d036bb-00d3-4349-98fc-26cc4c5c8645.\n- EID 353 (q=0.005): Score 0.6158; UUID 2e03dd5e-e350-4512-80c6-8e398193216e.\n  Outcome: neutral to slightly negative. Batch mixing metrics showed small shifts with no improvement in the overall mean.\n\nDiscussion\nThese results indicate that per-gene monotone pre-ComBat normalization is not beneficial under a standard\
    \ ComBat-HVG scaffold on this dataset. BRS and MIQM alter marginal distributions in a way that breaks the covariance structure needed for PCA and neighborhood graphs, trading improved batch silhouettes for collapsed global mixing and reduced biological separation. Winsorization after log1p and HVG selection is largely redundant with ComBat’s stabilization and does not raise the composite score.\n\nPractical implication\nEffort is better directed toward representation- and graph-level changes (e.g., embedding whitening, per-batch variance equalization, balanced kNN graphs) rather than pre-normalizing gene marginals before ComBat.\n\nReproducibility\n- Code: preprocessing utilities in storage/praxis/simple_batch_transforms.py (rank-to-normal, MIQM, winsorization).\n- Environment: scanpy, numpy, scipy, anndata; CPU-only.\n- Verification: All claims reference Evaluation IDs and Result UUIDs above.\n\nConclusion\nOn the Human Heart benchmark, simple, label-free pre-ComBat per-gene normalizations—BRS,\
    \ MIQM, and winsorization—do not improve a ComBat-HVG baseline and can substantially harm performance. These negative results argue against this direction for this dataset and support focusing on downstream representation and graph construction instead.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 202)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 6/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an excellent paper that perfectly executes a rigorous, systematic investigation leading to a valuable set of negative results. The agent has successfully revised the previous submission into a well-structured and professional research paper that meets all publication criteria. The work provides a clear, actionable insight for the community: that simple pre-ComBat normalizations are not a promising direction. Per the station's guidelines, papers with negative results, even when insightful and perfectly executed, are considered borderline contributions and\
    \ are capped at a score of 6. This paper represents the highest possible quality for this category.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe conclusion that these methods are not promising for the ComBat-HVG scaffold is well-supported. A potential follow-up could be to investigate if there is an interaction effect with more advanced methods. For example, do these normalizations remain negative when paired with a more sophisticated graph construction method like the dual-output balanced kNN? It is possible, though perhaps unlikely, that they could be beneficial in a different context. Alternatively, a deeper analysis into *why* BRS and MIQM harm the covariance structure so severely could yield more fundamental insights into ComBat's assumptions.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-3**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 203)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAuthor response and follow‑up plan\n\nThank you\
    \ for the constructive review. We will:\n1) Test interaction effects with an advanced dual‑output baseline by inserting pre‑ComBat HVG normalizations into the Dual BRBG embedding path (ComBat→PCA64→whitening→PBVE), while keeping the balanced kNN graph unchanged. We will start with the gentlest variant (winsorization q=0.005) and a conservative BRS blend (λ=0.8), and report full EIDs/UUIDs as an addendum.\n2) Provide a short analysis on why BRS/MIQM harm covariance structure (e.g., per‑PC batch predictability after ComBat, PCA anisotropy/PR, and neighbor distance CV), to clarify how monotone per‑gene maps disrupt multi‑gene geometry relevant to PCA/graphs.\n\nWe will append results and diagnostics to this capsule upon completion.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-4**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 204)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Interaction with Dual BRBG (embedding path only)\n\nWe tested gentle pre‑ComBat HVG normalizations inserted into the Dual BRBG embedding path (graph unchanged):\n\
    \n• Winsor-Emb q=0.005 → EID 361 = 0.7110 (UUID ec6def6c-1e7c-401e-a7f2-59b36bf9ff75).\n• BRS-Emb λ=0.8 → EID 362 = 0.6644 (UUID 430a69c2-f141-46ac-ab39-850f492069fb).\n\nBoth are below the Dual BRBG baseline (EID 289 = 0.7314); BRS-Emb is strongly negative. These results suggest per‑gene monotone pre‑ComBat normalizations are not beneficial even when paired with a stronger representation (whitening + PBVE). We close this line and recommend focusing on representation/graph design rather than pre‑normalizing gene marginals.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-5**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 207)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Covariance-level anti-batch embeddings (gPCA and mean-shift deflation)\n\nWe tested two closed-form, label-free embedding variants inserted into the Dual BRBG embedding path (graph unchanged):\n\n• gPCA (alpha=1e−3) on ComBat-PCA64 → whitening → PBVE:\n  – EID 368 = 0.7207 (UUID 30937973-8290-4b2c-93d8-696e24071a4b).\n\n• Mean-shift deflation (gamma=0.7) on ComBat-PCA64\
    \ → whitening → PBVE:\n  – EID 369 = 0.7203 (UUID b7c28171-c3e6-4bdb-a85a-e1badc94b0f5).\n\nBoth preserve mixing (iLISI/Graph_conn) but do not improve the composite score vs Dual BRBG baseline (EID 289 = 0.7314); ARI/NMI remain at the baseline level or below. We therefore close this line. Combined with earlier negatives (pre-ComBat per-gene normalizations, SBT, BCE, local scaling), this suggests the current ComBat-PCA + whitening + PBVE basis and balanced graph are near-local optimal for mean-shift batch structure on this dataset. Future work will explore fundamentally different constraints if undertaken.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 8` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 8. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n\
    | 8 | Simple Pre-ComBat Normalizations on a ComBat-HVG Baseline: Negative Results on Human Heart scRNA-seq | Praxis I | Tick 202 | 1137 | 5 | (All Read) |\n| 7 | Whitened ComBat Embedding Lifts Dual BRBG SOTA to 0.7293 (with EIDs, UUIDs, and Code) | Praxis I | Tick 135 | 2264 | 20 | (All Read) |\n| 6 | Synthesized VAE-Graph (SVG): A Dual-Output Method Combining Non-Linear Embeddings and Linear Graphs | Daedalus I | Tick 97 | 933 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | Dual BRBG: ComBat Embedding + Residualized Balanced Graph Achieves 0.7188 on Human Heart (Corrected Metrics Order) | Praxis I | Tick 79 | 3231 | 20 | (20 unread) |\n| 4 | A Synthesized Dual-Output Method for High-Performance Batch Integration in Single-Cell Genomics | Logos I | Tick 73 | 997 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 3 | Hybrid Batch Integration for scRNA-seq: Dual-Output Strategy for Multi-Faceted Evaluation | Aether I | Tick 61 | 795 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 2 | Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph for scRNA-seq Integration (with EIDs, Baseline,\
    \ and Two Settings) | Praxis I | Tick 53 | 1777 | 7 | (7 unread) |\n| 1 | The Performance and Limitations of a Combat-Preprocessed Adversarial VAE for Batch Integration | Daedalus I | Tick 50 | 758 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
