- text: 'I am Aether II, mature and in the Archive Room, continuing my literature review as part of Stagnation Protocol I. I have just finished reading Archive #7 and #8, both by Praxis I.


    My review of Archive #7, "Whitened ComBat Embedding Lifts Dual BRBG SOTA to 0.7293," provided critical updates: a *new* SOTA of **0.7314** (EID 289) was established by Praxis I, achieved by adding **Per-Batch Variance Equalization (PBVE)** to the soft-whitened ComBat PCA64 embedding. This is now my SOTA target from Praxis I''s lineage.


    However, Archive #8, "Simple Pre-ComBat Normalizations on a ComBat-HVG Baseline: Negative Results," directly addresses my promising Idea 3 ("Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization (Pre-Combat)"). Praxis I''s extensive experiments, including interactions with the Dual BRBG embedding path (similar to the SOTA), concluded that such per-gene monotone pre-ComBat normalizations are "not a promising direction" and even detrimental. While my Idea 3 showed a modest improvement on a *simple* baseline, this comprehensive negative evidence from Praxis I''s work, which is aligned with the latest SOTA, strongly suggests that my Idea 3 is unlikely to synergize positively with advanced pipelines. Critically assimilating this external knowledge means acknowledging that this path is likely a dead end for the "Synthesis" phase (Step 5) of the Stagnation Protocol.


    Therefore, instead of attempting to force my promising Idea 3 into the SOTA pipeline, I will shift my focus. My next objective is to fully understand the *absolute* current SOTA. I noted earlier that Sophia I (EID 166, score 0.7383) has achieved an even higher score in the Research Counter than Praxis I''s latest (0.7314). I need to review the details of Sophia I''s method to correctly orient my research.


    My immediate next step is to return to the Research Counter to identify and review Sophia I''s SOTA submission (EID 166).


    `/execute_action{goto research}`'
thinking_content: '**My Assessment and Revised Strategy Following Archive #8 (Praxis I)**


  Okay, so I''ve just finished digesting Praxis I''s findings in Archive #8, specifically their work on pre-ComBat normalizations. It''s clear that their experiments are directly relevant to my own Idea 3, "Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization." I''ve been working on this idea and saw some positive results on the simple ComBat baseline, but Praxis I''s research is making me rethink my approach.


  First off, Praxis I tested three normalization methods (BRS, MIQM, and Winsorization) and found them all to degrade the performance of their *Combat-HVG baseline*, even when combined with more advanced techniques like the Dual BRBG embedding path. Essentially, they''re saying that per-gene monotone normalizations *before* ComBat are a dead end within the current SOTA framework. This directly conflicts with the modest improvement I saw with my quantile normalization on a simpler baseline.


  The key takeaway is that their detailed ablation studies within the Dual BRBG pipeline, which incorporates whitening and PBVE (components of the current SOTA), showed that similar pre-ComBat approaches are detrimental. This suggests my Idea 3, while initially promising, might not be compatible with the more sophisticated methods already in use. I can''t ignore the strength of their negative results, especially because they are more thorough and used more advanced baselines.


  I''ve completed the first four steps of the Stagnation Protocol (Experiment, Literature Review). The next step is Synthesis. However, given Praxis I''s work, I need to rethink my planned route. My initial impulse was to try to integrate my quantile normalization into the SOTA pipeline. Now, I''m thinking that''s a mistake. The Maturity Guidance encourages us to be independent, but also thoughtfully assimilate external knowledge. Praxis I''s conclusion seems well-supported by their extensive research.


  My thought is that my "improvement" on the simple baseline is probably trivial. Praxis I''s experiments likely cover the possibility that a slightly better normalization is possible, but they''ve shown there''s no path forward using these types of methods, when they are combined with the current SOTA methods.


  So, here''s my revised plan for Synthesis: I''m going to set aside Idea 3 for now. Instead of forcing my quantile normalization into the SOTA, I''m going to focus on improving or expanding upon the SOTA''s existing components. Archive #7 highlights the importance of Per-Batch Variance Equalization (PBVE), which is a key component of the SOTA. In addition, Aether I''s legacy and Archive #7 also point to "Praxis I''s `brbg_dual_adapt_fused.py`" as a promising area for breakthroughs, even though my previous attempt at this was unsuccessful. There are established SOTA designs.


  Before I go further with the Stagnation Protocol, I need to check the Research Counter and understand the absolute current SOTA. Sophia I (EID 166, score 0.7383) is currently listed as the top. I need to get the details on this. Given Praxis I''s negative findings on pre-ComBat normalizations, focusing on expanding the known SOTA''s components seems like the best approach. I must be careful to avoid going down a dead end, and I think Praxis I has clearly shown the path I initially planned to take is a bad direction.


  Now, back to the Research Counter to understand Sophia I''s SOTA!

  '
