role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aether II, currently in the Research Counter at 79 ticks, mature and following Stagnation Protocol I.\n\nI have completed the literature review and identified Daedalus II's \"Embed then Correct\" (EtC) with BRBG (EID 378, score 0.7355) as the new highest *credible* SOTA. I am now in the \"Experiment\" phase (Step 4), starting with the reproduction of this baseline. I have just reviewed EID 378 and obtained its code.\n\nTo ensure a self-contained and accurate replication, I will embed the necessary helper functions (which are common across many top-performing methods from other lineages and were implicitly part of prior SOTA submissions I reviewed) directly into my submission. The core logic of the `eliminate_batch_effect_fn` will faithfully mirror Daedalus II's EID 378, particularly the `n_comps=60` for PCA in the embedding path. I will also ensure a consistent `random_state` for reproducibility.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Aether II SOTA Replication:\
    \ Daedalus II's Embed-then-Correct (EtC) with BRBG (n_comps=60)\"\ntags: \"sota-replication, embed-then-correct, dual-output, brbg, stagnation-protocol, baseline-reproduction\"\nabstract: \"This submission aims to precisely replicate Daedalus II's SOTA (EID 378, score 0.7355). It implements the 'Embed then Correct' (EtC) method with PCA to 60 components followed by ComBat correction in PC space for the embedding, combined with the standard Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph (BRBG) for graph construction. This replication serves as the foundational step for further experimentation within Stagnation Protocol I.\"\ncontent: |\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.decomposition import TruncatedSVD\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  # --- Helper functions (from common SOTA logic, e.g., Praxis I's auto_gamma_brbg.py, or Daedalus's sophia_sota_logic.py) ---\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata:\
    \ ad.AnnData, target_sum: float = 1e4):\n      \"\"\"Normalize raw counts and log1p in-place.\"\"\"\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers: adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _get_hvg_mask(adata: ad.AnnData):\n      \"\"\"Use batch-aware HVGs if provided, otherwise compute HVGs with batch_key.\"\"\"\n      if 'batch_hvg' in adata.var.columns:\n          mask = np.asarray(adata.var['batch_hvg'].values, dtype=bool)\n          if mask.sum() > 0:\n              return mask\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, n_top_genes=2000, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', inplace=True)\n      return np.asarray(adata.var['highly_variable'].values, dtype=bool)\n\n  def _pca_truncated_svd(X, n_comps=50, random_state=0):\n      \"\"\"Sparse-aware PCA via TruncatedSVD.\"\"\"\n      # Ensure n_comps is valid: at least 2, and less than the number of features if X is not empty\n      n_comps\
    \ = max(2, min(n_comps, X.shape[1]-1 if X.shape[1] > 1 else 2))\n      svd = TruncatedSVD(n_components=n_comps, n_iter=7, random_state=random_state)\n      Z = svd.fit_transform(X)\n      return Z\n\n  def _one_hot_batches(batches: np.ndarray):\n      \"\"\"One-hot encode batch labels.\"\"\"\n      batches = np.asarray(batches)\n      uniq, inv = np.unique(batches, return_inverse=True)\n      B = np.zeros((batches.shape[0], len(uniq)), dtype=np.float32)\n      B[np.arange(batches.shape[0]), inv] = 1.0\n      return B, uniq\n\n  def _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B: np.ndarray, Z: np.ndarray, l2: float = 1e-3):\n      \"\"\"\n      Ridge fit batch one-hot B to predict PCs Z. Returns Z_hat (n x d) and W (b x d).\n      \"\"\"\n      BtB = B.T @ B\n      # Add small regularization to diagonal to prevent singular matrix issues if batches are degenerate\n      W = np.linalg.solve(BtB + l2 * np.eye(BtB.shape[0], dtype=np.float32), B.T @ Z)\n      Z_hat = B @ W\n      return Z_hat, W\n\n  def _build_balanced_knn(Z:\
    \ np.ndarray, batches: np.ndarray, k_total=48, metric='cosine'):\n      \"\"\"\n      Build a batch-balanced kNN graph:\n        - Equal per-batch quotas (distributes remainder across batches in order)\n        - Binary connectivities (1.0), symmetric union\n        - Distances from per-batch neighbor queries (min distance when duplicate)\n      Returns (C, D) as CSR matrices.\n      \"\"\"\n      Z = np.asarray(Z, dtype=np.float32)\n      batches = np.asarray(batches)\n      uniq_batches = np.unique(batches)\n      n = Z.shape[0]\n      nb = len(uniq_batches)\n      if nb == 0 or n == 0:\n          return None, None\n\n      base = k_total // nb\n      rem = k_total - base * nb\n      quotas = {b: base + (1 if i < rem else 0) for i, b in enumerate(uniq_batches)}\n\n      pairs_min_dist = {}\n      for b in uniq_batches:\n          idx_b = np.where(batches == b)[0]\n          if idx_b.size == 0 or quotas[b] == 0:\n              continue\n          k = min(quotas[b] + 1, idx_b.size) \
    \ # +1 to allow for removing self-neighbor\n          nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k, metric=metric, algorithm='auto')\n          nn.fit(Z[idx_b])\n          dists, inds = nn.kneighbors(Z, n_neighbors=k, return_distance=True)\n          neigh_global = idx_b[inds]\n          for i in range(n):\n              g = neigh_global[i]\n              di = dists[i]\n              mask = g != i # Mask out self-neighbor\n              g = g[mask][:quotas[b]]\n              di = di[mask][:quotas[b]]\n              for j, d in zip(g, di):\n                  if i == j: # Double-check for self-neighbor after masking\n                      continue\n                  a, c = (i, j) if i < j else (j, i) # Canonical ordering\n                  prev = pairs_min_dist.get((a, c))\n                  if (prev is None) or (d < prev):\n                      pairs_min_dist[(a, c)] = float(d)\n\n      if not pairs_min_dist:\n          return None, None\n\n      rows, cols, dist_data = [], [], []\n      for\
    \ (i, j), d in pairs_min_dist.items():\n          rows.append(i); cols.append(j); dist_data.append(d)\n          rows.append(j); cols.append(i); dist_data.append(d)\n\n      from scipy.sparse import coo_matrix\n      D = coo_matrix((dist_data, (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      C = D.copy()\n      C.data[:] = 1.0  # binary connectivities\n      return C, D\n\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      print(\"Aether II: Replication of Daedalus II SOTA (EID 378) - Embed-then-Correct (EtC) with BRBG (n_comps=60)...\")\n      \n      random_state = 0 # For reproducibility\n\n      adata_for_graph = adata.copy()\n      adata_for_embedding = adata.copy()\n\n      # --- EMBEDDING PATH: \"Embed then Correct\" with n_comps=60 ---\n      print(\"Embedding Path: Running PCA(n=60) then ComBat...\")\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata_for_embedding, target_sum=1e4)\n      # HVGs are selected here, but ComBat in PC space doesn't explicitly filter genes,\n\
    \      # it operates on the PCs derived from HVGs. Daedalus II's EID 378 computes HVGs\n      # on adata_for_embedding then runs PCA, so we follow that flow.\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata_for_embedding, n_top_genes=2000, batch_key='batch', flavor='seurat_v3', inplace=True)\n      \n      # Use subset=True here to ensure PCA runs only on HVGs as implied by the pipeline.\n      sc.pp.pca(adata_for_embedding, n_comps=60, use_highly_variable=True, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      \n      # Create a temporary AnnData object for ComBat on PCs\n      pca_adata = ad.AnnData(X=adata_for_embedding.obsm['X_pca'], obs=adata_for_embedding.obs)\n      sc.pp.combat(pca_adata, key='batch')\n      Z_emb = pca_adata.X\n      \n      # --- GRAPH PATH: Standard BRBG pipeline ---\n      print(\"Graph Path: Running standard BRBG...\")\n      n_pcs_graph = 50\n      gamma_max = 0.74\n      l2 = 1e-3\n      lam = 0.9 # Used in original Praxis I's adaptive residualization.\n\
    \      k_total = 48\n      metric = 'cosine'\n      \n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata_for_graph, target_sum=1e4)\n      hvg_mask = _get_hvg_mask(adata_for_graph) # Use helper function to get HVG mask\n      \n      X_graph = adata_for_graph.X[:, hvg_mask]\n      if not sp.issparse(X_graph): X_graph = sp.csr_matrix(X_graph)\n      Z_graph = _pca_truncated_svd(X_graph, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=random_state)\n      \n      batches = np.asarray(adata_for_graph.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n      B_onehot, _ = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Z_hat, _ = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B_onehot, Z_graph, l2=l2)\n      \n      # Adaptive residualization logic from Praxis I\n      var_total = Z_graph.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      var_pred = (Z_hat.var(axis=0, ddof=1)).clip(min=0.0)\n      R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0)\n      gamma_vec = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2)\n      \n      Z_corr_graph = Z_graph - (Z_hat * gamma_vec)\n      C, D = _build_balanced_knn(Z_corr_graph.astype(np.float32),\
    \ batches=batches, k_total=k_total, metric=metric)\n      \n      # --- Final Output ---\n      adata_out = ad.AnnData(\n          obs=adata.obs[[]], \n          var=adata.var[[]], \n          obsm={'X_emb': Z_emb}, # Set the embedding\n          uns={\n              'method_id': 'aether_ii_etc_brbg_replication_n60',\n              'etc_brbg_config': {\n                  'n_comps_emb': int(60),\n                  'n_pcs_graph': int(n_pcs_graph),\n                  'gamma_max': float(gamma_max),\n                  'l2': float(l2),\n                  'lam': float(lam),\n                  'k_total': int(k_total),\n                  'metric': metric,\n                  'replicated_sota_eid': 378\n              }\n          }\n      )\n      \n      if C is not None and D is not None:\n          adata_out.obsp['connectivities'] = C.tocsr()\n          adata_out.obsp['distances'] = D.tocsr()\n          adata_out.uns['neighbors'] = {'connectivities_key': 'connectivities', 'distances_key': 'distances'}\n\
    \          \n      print(f\"✓ Aether II: Replication of Daedalus II SOTA (EID 378) complete.\")\n      return adata_out\n```"
thinking_content: "**Daedalus II Replication: Preparing the Submission**\n\nOkay, so the plan is clear: replicate Daedalus II's SOTA (EID 378). This is the first step of my \"Experiment\" phase in Stagnation Protocol I. I've just reviewed EID 378 and I have the code right here. \n\nFirst things first, I need to get this code submitted. I'll make absolutely sure the YAML is formatted correctly. Then, I need to deal with those helper functions. EID 378 imports `_get_hvg_mask, _pca_truncated_svd, _one_hot_batches, _ridge_batch_fit_predict, _build_balanced_knn` from `storage/Daedalus/sophia_sota_logic.py`. But wait... I recognize these! These are the same utilities used in Praxis I's previous SOTA (EID 289) and Sophia I (EID 166).  I *do* already have these helpers, either from my Sophia I replication (EID 390) or directly from the Praxis I submission.  \n\nRather than mess around with imports from a potentially unfamiliar file, I'll just embed them directly into my submission.  It's cleaner,\
  \ more self-contained, and I know these functions like the back of my hand anyway. They were part of the EID 289 review, after all. \n\nSo, here's what the submission will look like:\n\n1.  *Everything* from `sophia_sota_logic.py` gets included directly. No external imports, no dependencies. Self-contained goodness.\n2.  The core logic, especially that crucial `eliminate_batch_effect_fn`, will *exactly* match Daedalus II's EID 378.  I'll pay extra attention to that `n_comps=60` in the PCA embedding path. It's a key detail.\n3.  I'll set `random_state` everywhere I can to ensure reproducibility. This is always important.\n\nToken budget? Right, I should be mindful of that. But I'm currently at 77% after the pruning, so I should be fine for this submission. Let's get this done!\n"
