    *   **Archive #12** highlights the importance of component compatibility, showing that even an optimized embedding might not synergize with a BRBG graph. This resonates with my challenges in integrating modifications into the established EtC-BRBG pipeline.

    *   **Archive #13** explicitly describes how Latent Correlation Minimization, while reducing linear batch correlation, *detrimentally impacted local batch mixing metrics* like ASW_batch and iLISI. This is a direct parallel to the failure modes observed in my own experiments (EID 434, where iLISI dropped to 0.000). This provides strong external validation for the difficulty of modifying foundational integration processes without unintended negative consequences, especially for local structure.


    These insights will be crucial for drafting my report as per Stagnation Protocol I (Step 6), allowing me to provide a more informed discussion of why my ideas failed. I will now read these two papers in full.


    `/execute_action{read 12,13}`'
thinking_content: '**Contextualizing My Setbacks**


  Okay, so I just took a quick look at Archives #12 and #13, and things are starting to click. Archive #12, "Veritas I," highlights a compatibility issue when combining Harmony with BRBG – a negative synergy. That''s immediately relevant because I''ve been grappling with similar challenges in my own experiments. The problems sound similar to the issues I''ve been seeing with my own process.


  Then, Archive #13, "Sophia II," really caught my eye. They were looking at using Latent Correlation Minimization (LCCM) as a post-processing step after ComBat-PCA, and it ended up *hurting* their batch mixing metrics. This is huge! My own attempts to improve batch mixing have been consistently failing, and my "iLISI" scores are just...well, disappointing. This gives me a clear idea that I am not alone in this sort of failure.


  I''m in the "Report" phase now (Step 6 of Stagnation Protocol I), and having failed with all three of my novel ideas in the EtC-BRBG SOTA, this new information is going to be incredibly valuable for framing my negative results. Understanding *why* methods fail in synergy, and specific failure modes, will help me explain my own setbacks more clearly and insightfully.


  Now, I''m going to dig into Archives #12 and #13 properly. I want to thoroughly understand these cases, because their discussions on method compatibility and potential pitfalls in embedding modifications are directly relevant to my situation. This is going to help me ensure my report offers as much depth and insight as possible.

  '
