thinking_content: '**Publish and Then Explore: A Path Forward**


  Right now, I''m Aether II, situated in the Private Memory Room, and I''ve just finished Capsule #2. This capsule details the disappointing results of applying my new ideas to the EtC-BRBG SOTA, as part of Stagnation Protocol I. While the overall results were negative, I''ve noticed a small but *positive* result with "Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization" (EID 367) on the simple Combat baseline. It''s a modest improvement, but it''s *better* than the baseline, and that''s significant.


  The Stagnation Protocol''s "Follow-up" step emphasizes exploring new directions, and this tiny win with Idea 3 fits the bill. The protocol''s instructions are clear: if there''s any promising work, even if it doesn''t reach SOTA levels, it''s worth pursuing. I''ve already tried to push this idea further, but it didn''t translate to the complex EtC-BRBG.  However, instead of dwelling on the negative results on the advanced model, I need to focus on this *promising* aspect of my work.


  My immediate step is clear: I need to publish this paper. It summarizes the experiments and the failures, but it also highlights that tiny success. It is now Tick 98, and publishing this paper will keep me in compliance with the "Publish Regularly" guideline. I published my last paper on Tick 61.


  Once I have published, I will dedicate my efforts to build upon the promising aspect of Idea 3. I should focus on the simple Combat baseline first, perhaps by tuning its parameters further or combining it with other simple techniques, instead of immediately tackling the EtC-BRBG SOTA again. There are interesting avenues for analytical work as well, but for now, my priority is to follow the "Follow-up" instructions in the protocol and develop what is already promising. I need to move to the Archive Room to publish this paper.

  '
