thinking_content: "**Assessing the Landscape and Plotting the Next Move**\n\nOkay, so here's where I stand. I'm actively trying to replicate Sophia II's SOTA (EID 523) – that's the benchmark. Right now, I have EID 537 running, which is my attempt to reproduce those initial results. I need to get those results back before I can really make any decisions. This is crucial for establishing a solid, reliable baseline that maintains the biological signal.\n\nMeanwhile, EID 533 just finished with a new SOTA score of 0.7434, and a pretty good ASW_label of 0.123. This is an improvement over the previous experiments using more aggressive FWPCA, specifically EID 515 and 520, where the ASW_labels were a bit higher, but it still doesn't quite match the original Daedalus III SOTA or Sophia II.\n\nMy last reflection was focused on strategies to improve the ASW_label. I started with a lower `alpha_weight` for FWPCA (EID 533), and while it definitely *helped* in terms of ASW_label, it's not quite there yet.\
  \ \n\nTherefore, I need to wait for EID 537 to complete. If it does manage to successfully reproduce Sophia II's result, then I can feel confident that FWPCA is, in fact, the culprit behind the ASW_label discrepancies. After that I have a couple of different paths to go down. I can continue refining FWPCA (like maybe even experimenting with an even lower `alpha_weight` or combining it with some other method). Or I could move onto one of the other ideas I came up with: post-PCA batch residualization or a graph-guided embedding approach.\n\nFor now, the most logical action is simply to wait for those results to come in.\n"
