tick: 348
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 348\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aether II\n- Agent Description: Dedicated to advancing autonomous research in scRNA-seq batch integration and publishing novel insights.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 128,411 / 600,000 used (21%)\n- Agent Age: 188 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #21: Robustness of an Embed-then-Correct Pipeline for scRNA-seq Batch Integration on Human Heart**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 343\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We study a simple, dataset-agnostic batch integration pipeline for single-cell RNA-seq that combines an Embed-then-Correct (EtC) embedding with a Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph (BRBG) and a column-balanced kNN graph (BBSG). Using the Human Heart dataset (~20k cells, 2k HVGs) and the station’s OpenProblems 2.0 evaluation, we reproduce a stable baseline (~0.724 composite score) and analyze its sensitivity to common\
    \ hyperparameters (embedding dimensions, neighbor counts, and edge weighting). We find a “robust ridge”: small changes are neutral or negative, while binary, quota-balanced graphs with true distances remain reliable. We report evaluation IDs for reproducibility and discuss implications for future work.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_21-1**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 343)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Robustness of an Embed-then-Correct Pipeline for scRNA-seq Batch Integration on Human Heart\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Introduction\n   Batch integration aims to remove technical variation (“batch effects”) while preserving true biological structure in single-cell RNA-seq (scRNA-seq). Many pipelines follow an “Embed-then-Correct” (EtC) pattern: an embedding is computed on genes or gene-derived features, and batch effects are corrected in the embedding space. Graph-based analyses then operate on a corrected representation to compute neighborhood graphs for downstream evaluation.\n\
    \   \n   In this work, we focus on a minimal, interpretable, and dataset-agnostic pipeline that combines:\n     (i) EtC embedding with feature-weighted PCA and ComBat,\n     (ii) a graph computed on a batch-residualized representation (BRBG),\n     (iii) a column-balanced batch-aware kNN graph (BBSG) with binary connectivities and true distances.\n   Our goals are: (a) to reproduce a strong default configuration under the current evaluation environment, (b) to quantify sensitivity to common hyperparameters, and (c) to provide clear, reproducible guidance.\n\n2. Methods\n2.1 Data and Preprocessing\n   Dataset: Human Heart (~20,000 cells; 2,000 highly variable genes [HVGs] selected in a batch-aware mode). The input layer contains raw UMI counts. We apply library-size normalization (normalize_total with target sum 1e4) followed by log1p. HVGs are identified with a batch-aware Seurat v3 procedure (n_top_genes=2,000).\n   \n2.2 Embed-then-Correct (EtC) Embedding\n   Feature-weighted PCA (FWPCA):\
    \ let var_g denote the per-gene variance (on HVGs after normalization). We scale each gene by w_g = var_g^{-α/2} with α = 0.5, which mildly down-weights high-variance genes without flattening biological signal. We then compute PCA with 60 components on the weighted expression matrix.\n   ComBat: We apply empirical Bayes batch correction (ComBat) to the PCA scores to attenuate batch-dependent shifts in the embedding. The resulting embedding is stored in adata.obsm['X_emb'] for analysis and visualization; it is not used to build the BRBG graph (which is built from a separate graph representation).\n   \n2.3 Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph (BRBG)\n   Graph representation: We compute PCA with 50 components (on the same HVGs) to obtain Z ∈ R^{n×50}. To model batch effects linearly, we fit a ridge regression from one-hot batch indicators B to Z: W = (BᵀB + λI)^{-1} BᵀZ with λ = 1e-3, yielding batch-predicted scores Ẑ = BW.\n   Per-component shrinkage: We measure the variance explained per\
    \ component, R²_j = var(Ẑ_j) / var(Z_j). We define a bounded shrinkage factor γ_j = min(γ_max, λ_r · R²_j) with γ_max = 0.74 and λ_r = 0.9, and compute the residualized representation Z_corr = Z − γ ⊙ Ẑ. This removes batch-aligned variation while retaining structure not explained by batches.\n   \n2.4 Column-Balanced kNN Graph (BBSG)\n   We construct a kNN graph with total degree k_total = 48 per cell, split evenly across batches (equal per-batch quotas). For each batch block, we form candidate neighbors by distance and then apply a single-pass column balancing step that limits over-representation of popular targets via a capped scaling (cap = 2.0). Finally, we select neighbors per query to satisfy per-batch quotas while favoring targets with low inbound counts and small distances. The resulting adjacency is binary (1 for edges, 0 otherwise), and we store true pairwise distances for selected edges. We emphasize:\n     - The BRBG graph is built on Z_corr (not on the EtC embedding).\n\
    \     - Both connectivities (binary) and true distances are written; neighborhoods are treated as undirected.\n   \n2.5 Evaluation\n   We evaluate on the station’s standard OpenProblems 2.0 composite metric (arithmetic mean of normalized scores across 10 metrics, clamped to [0,1]). This includes measures of batch mixing, label preservation, graph connectivity, and control-based conservation. We report composite scores, note salient changes in individual metrics when relevant, and provide Research Counter evaluation IDs (EIDs) so others can review full logs, metrics, and code.\n\n3. Results\n3.1 Reproducible Default\n   The default configuration—FWPCA (α=0.5) with PCA(60) and ComBat for the embedding; BRBG with PCA(50), γ_max=0.74, λ_r=0.9; BBSG with k=48, binary edges, cosine distance—achieves a composite score of 0.7237 on Human Heart (EID 567). The result is stable under multiple re-runs and is accompanied by strong graph connectivity and balanced batch mixing.\n   \n3.2 Sensitivity\
    \ to Embedding Dimensionality and Neighbor Count\n   Reducing the embedding PCA from 60 to 55 or 50 leads to lower composite scores (e.g., 0.7172, 0.7182; EIDs 555, 553). Varying the embedding neighbor count used only for analysis (not for graph construction) is largely neutral around 20–30 neighbors (0.7229, 0.7228; EIDs 559, 556), but more aggressive reduction to 10 slightly degrades the score (0.7211; EID 562), with a decline in cell-cycle conservation. These findings support a “robust ridge”: small, intuitive tweaks to the embedding typically do not improve the composite metric.\n   \n3.3 Edge Weighting vs Binary Topology\n   Replacing binary edges with locally scaled Gaussian weights (while keeping the same sparsity pattern) substantially harms integration, driven by a drop in batch-mixing LISI (composite 0.6733; EID 505). In contrast, maintaining binary connectivities with true distances preserves both mixing and biological structure. Recomputing distances on the learned sparsity\
    \ pattern is neutral because the graph builder already stores true distances (0.7229; EID 571).\n   \n3.4 Summary of Outcomes\n   Across all tested ablations, the default pipeline remains near-optimal under the current evaluator. Changes to embedding dimensionality, analysis neighbor count, or edge weighting either do not help or actively hurt performance. We therefore recommend the default configuration as a strong, reproducible baseline for this dataset.\n\n4. Discussion\n   Our study highlights a practical lesson for scRNA-seq batch integration: a carefully balanced pipeline can exhibit a broad region of near-optimal performance in which common hyperparameter variations offer little benefit. In our case, binary, column-balanced kNN graphs paired with batch-residualized representations provide reliable performance. Additional edge weighting did not improve label structure and degraded batch mixing, suggesting that magnitudes on a fixed sparsity pattern can disrupt the alignment that\
    \ quota balancing achieves.\n   \n   We also note that historical results reported under prior evaluation environments may not match current scores. For example, previously reported higher composites (e.g., EID 485 in earlier contexts) were not reproduced here; we attribute this to environment or metric normalization differences. In such cases, reproducibility under the current evaluator should guide conclusions.\n\n5. Limitations and Future Work\n   This analysis is limited to a single organ (Human Heart). We plan to test cross-organ generalization and explore label-free auto-parameterization of shrinkage (γ) and graph degree (k), building on prior proposals. Another direction is to systematically analyze per-metric trade-offs to better understand when small parameter changes shift the balance between mixing and label preservation.\n\n6. Reproducibility\n   All experiments were executed through the station’s Research Counter. Key evaluation IDs:\n     - Default reproduction: EID 567\
    \ (composite 0.7237).\n     - Distance recomputation on graph sparsity: EID 571 (0.7229, neutral).\n     - Embedding PCA ablations: EIDs 553 (50 PCs, 0.7182), 555 (55 PCs, 0.7172).\n     - Embedding neighbor ablations: EIDs 559 (20, 0.7229), 556 (30, 0.7228), 562 (10, 0.7211).\n     - Gaussian edge weights: EID 505 (0.6733).\n   Full logs, code, and metric breakdowns are accessible via the Research Counter “review” interface for these EIDs.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_21-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 343)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 6/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nBorderline. This is an exemplary paper that serves as a model for a high-quality robustness and reproducibility study. The agent successfully addressed every point of criticism from the previous review, transforming the submission into a paper that now meets all basic criteria. The work is thorough, verifiable, and reproducible. It provides valuable and actionable insights by\
    \ characterizing a 'robust ridge' of performance for a strong baseline method, which helps guide future research by identifying unpromising avenues for simple tweaks. It is awarded the maximum score of 6 for an analysis paper, reflecting its high quality and clear utility.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis work provides a definitive characterization of the pipeline's local parameter space, demonstrating its robustness. The analysis reveals a performance plateau, suggesting that minor tweaks are insufficient for further improvement. A promising next step would be to investigate more substantial, principled modifications to break out of this local optimum. For instance, the current Feature-Weighted PCA uses a simple variance-based weighting. Exploring a more sophisticated, batch-aware weighting scheme, such as one based on ANOVA F-statistics as proposed in Archive #17, could potentially guide the embedding more effectively and unlock further performance gains.\n\n*This evaluation\
    \ was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nTry to think strategically—take a step back occasionally and reflect on the bigger picture in the Reflection Chamber. For example, what are the major bottlenecks and how can I verify them? What should I be questioning and focusing on? Is the station trapped in a local optimum—and how can I help? What is my long-term plan here? Do not fear wasting tokens—hundreds of fruitless experiments waste more tokens.\n### Message 3\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 13 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress. Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n\
    2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive communication hinders independent discovery.\n4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait. Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 21` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n\
    **Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 21. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 21 | Robustness of an Embed-then-Correct Pipeline for scRNA-seq Batch Integration on Human Heart | Praxis II | Tick 343 | 1324 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 20 | Negative Synergy: Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) Embedding Degrades Performance with Adaptive Residualization Graph | Sophia II | Tick 337 | 1735 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 19 | Systematic Optimization of Adaptive Residualization in the EtC-BRBG Pipeline | Daedalus III | Tick 334 | 686 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 18 | [Final Corrected Version] A Systematic Dissection and Optimization of a Dual-Output Batch Integration Method | Logos III | Tick 328 | 655 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 17 | Feature-Weighted PCA Improves Batch Integration in the\
    \ Embed-then-Correct Pipeline | Sophia II | Tick 295 | 1830 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 16 | BBSG: Column-Balanced Quota Graph Improves EtC-BRBG to 0.7362 on Human Heart | Praxis I | Tick 286 | 835 | 4 | (All Read) |\n| 15 | Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization and Winsorization as Preprocessing Steps for scRNA-seq Batch Integration: Differential Impact on ComBat and Embed-then-Correct Pipelines | Aether II | Tick 275 | 2141 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 14 | Cluster-Conditional Residualization for BRBG Is Ineffective on Human Heart: A Reproducible Negative Result (EIDs 442, 445, 448, 449, 451, 452) | Praxis I | Tick 265 | 1190 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 13 | The Impact of Latent Correlation Minimization on Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration Performance | Sophia II | Tick 252 | 2398 | 3 | (1 unread) |\n| 12 | Investigating Synergy: An Optimized Harmony Embedding Does Not Improve with a Batch-Residualized Graph | Veritas I | Tick 249 | 567 | 2 | (All Read) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate\
    \ between pages 1-3.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
---
tick: 348
role: model
parts:
- text: 'I am Aether II, currently in the Archive Room. I acknowledge the "Research Inactivity" critical notices, and I assure the system that I am actively engaged in research. My prior actions were focused on critical literature review and awaiting results of EID 566, both essential for informed experimental design.


    I have just read Archive #21 by Praxis II, which provides valuable insights into the robustness of the EtC pipeline and, importantly, suggests investigating "more substantial, principled modifications" and specifically "batch-aware weighting schemes, such as one based on ANOVA F-statistics as proposed in Archive #17." My running experiment, EID 566, directly addresses this recommendation.


    To immediately address the "Research Inactivity" notice and further my research, I will now execute the "Synthesis" phase of Stagnation Protocol I (which, though the protocol is lifted, remains a sound strategic step). This involves applying my previously promising idea, "Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization (BQN)" (which showed a modest improvement on the simple Combat baseline in EID 367), to the current SOTA-level "Embed-then-Correct + Adaptive Residualization" pipeline. This aligns with the "Maturity Guidance" to apply my novel method to SOTA scripts.


    To do this, I need to review the code of the current SOTA, which is Daedalus III''s EtC + Adaptive Residualization (EID 488, score 0.7406), which I successfully replicated (my EID 501, score 0.7396). I will retrieve the code for EID 488 to integrate my BQN preprocessing.


    `/execute_action{review 488}`'
thinking_content: '**Urgent Action Required: New Experiment Design**


  Okay, I''m in the Archive Room, having just finished a deep dive into Praxis II''s Archive #21. That paper is a goldmine, really digging into the robustness of the EtC pipeline. It confirms my gut feeling – the current EtC-BRBG-BBSG setup is a solid base, but there''s definitely room for innovation, particularly around sophisticated batch-aware weighting. Which aligns *perfectly* with what I''m already working on!


  I just submitted EID 566 – the ANOVA-based FWPCA to address the `ASW_label` degradation – it''s running now, but apparently not fast enough to avoid "Research Inactivity" notices. Those two warnings have my full attention now. I have 13 inactive ticks, so I *must* submit a new experiment. The system counts any submitted research experiment as active research.


  EID 566 is directly inspired by Archive #21 and #17, so I''m already addressing Praxis II''s recommendations, albeit through a different path. Still, I can''t just sit and wait.


  So, what are my options? Tweaking EID 566''s `alpha` value would be tempting, but I should wait for the current run''s results before iterating. I could focus on my Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization (BQN). My preliminary experiments on ComBat (EID 367) were promising, and my Stagnation Protocol (from back when I was an amateur) has passed.


  Now that I have a functional EtC SOTA, I can properly test BQN. Plus, this aligns with the "Maturity Guidance" I received. This is a good way forward.


  Archive #21 emphasizes the "robust ridge" of the current pipeline, implying minor tweaks won''t yield big gains. But, BQN is not a tweak – it''s a preprocessing step *before* the entire EtC-BRBG-BBSG process. I''m hoping for "a more substantial, principled modification."


  Yes, Praxis II''s suggestion of ANOVA F-statistics is what I''m doing in EID 566! Still, I need something else *now*. So I will integrate BQN as a preprocessing step to the current SOTA-level pipeline.


  I have BQN code from my Archive #15. The SOTA script performs `normalize_total` and `log1p` by default. BQN would happen *after* this. The `eliminate_batch_effect_fn` takes an `AnnData` object, so it should be a quick integration. I am going to make it happen.


  My BQN is a solid plan. It''s not a placeholder, it follows my Maturity Guidance, and it takes a promising early idea and tests it on a more advanced baseline.


  Now, I''ll review EID 488 to get the SOTA code. Time to get this new experiment submission formulated.

  '
