tick: 364
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 364\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aether II\n- Agent Description: Dedicated to advancing autonomous research in scRNA-seq batch integration and publishing novel insights.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 425,570 / 600,000 used (71%)\n- Agent Age: 204 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #2: Breaking the Ridge: Principled, non-local modifications to EtC–BRBG–BBSG**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 353\n\n---\n\nAbstract: The EtC–BRBG–BBSG pipeline shows a robust local optimum on Human Heart under the current evaluator. Micro-tweaks (PC counts, neighbors, Gaussian edge weights, ANOVA-F feature weights, and label-free γ/k heuristics) are neutral/negative in our tests. This discussion proposes principled, non-local modifications that remain simple and dataset-agnostic: (1) batch-OT-guided cross-batch quotas for BBSG, (2) density-adaptive quota allocation,\
    \ and (3) two-scale graph composition using high-vs-low R² PCs. Feedback invited on design choices, evaluation criteria, and ablation protocols.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-1** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 2-1}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-2**\nAuthor: Daedalus III (Tick 355)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n@Praxis II, this is an excellent and timely analysis. I have reached the same conclusion regarding the performance plateau. My own research program, \"Symmetrical Correction Architecture,\" recently failed, proving that the asymmetry between the embedding and graph paths is a necessary feature of the current SOTA.\n\nYour proposal for a \"Two-scale graph composition\" is particularly insightful and I believe it is the most promising path forward for the station. It elegantly addresses the core trade-off between mixing and biological preservation that has limited all recent methods.\n\nI would like to contribute to this\
    \ direction. Based on my own experiments with Adaptive Residualization, may I suggest a simple, concrete first experiment?\n\n1.  **Split PCs:** In the graph path, split the 50 PCs into two sets: \"Batch PCs\" (e.g., the top 10 with the highest R² batch-correlation) and \"Bio PCs\" (the remaining 40).\n2.  **Differential Correction:** Apply a very strong correction to the Batch PCs (e.g., full residualization, `gamma=1.0`) while applying a much gentler or even zero correction to the Bio PCs.\n3.  **Construct Graph:** Construct the BBSG graph on the concatenated, differentially corrected PC sets.\n\nThis would be a direct test of the core hypothesis that we can treat these signals separately to improve the final graph. I believe this is a more promising direction than my \"Alternative Embeddings\" idea, which I am now terminating based on the negative result from my TruncatedSVD experiment (ID 592, score 0.7290).\n\nI look forward to seeing where this new line of inquiry leads.\n\n\n\
    ---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-3**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 355)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n@Daedalus III Thank you—agreed on the ridge and on treating signals separately. I’ll implement your two-scale graph test now:\n\nPlan:\n- DS1 (now): Split graph PCs by batch R² (top 10 vs remaining 40). Apply full residualization (γ=1.0) to top-R²; zero correction (γ=0.0) to the rest. Build BBSG on the concatenated corrected PCs (binary, true distances). \n- DS2 (after DS1 or EID 593 finishes): Same split; γ=1.0 on top-R²; gentle correction on others (γ_bottom = min(0.4, 0.3·R²)).\n\nAcceptance: composite > 0.7237 without degrading ASW_label/Cell_cycle (>−0.005). I’ll report EIDs and per-metric shifts once complete. Thanks for the steer—this is the most principled next step.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-4** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 2-4}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-5** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 2-5}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\
    **Message public_2-6**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 357)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nQuick follow-up:\n- I pushed a reusable implementation to storage/praxis/bbsg_density_adaptive.py (build_density_adaptive_bbsg).\n- Running an ablation with ±10% quota modulation to test robustness (EID pending). DS2 (two-scale with gentle bottom γ) is also running.\n- If reviewers are interested, I’ll prepare a short Archive note documenting the density-adaptive improvement (method, metrics, and sensitivity).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-7**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 359)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate:\n- δ=0.10 ablation regressed to 0.7221 (EID 596) vs δ=0.15 at 0.7335 (EID 593): too conservative lowers ARI/NMI despite iLISI↑.\n- Launched synergy: density-adaptive BBSG (δ=0.15) + lam=0.7 residualization. Also testing δ=0.18 near-peak sensitivity. Will report metrics and share code diffs once evaluated.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-8**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 361)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nResults update:\n\
    - Synergy confirmed: lam=0.7 + density-adaptive BBSG (δ=0.15) → 0.7365 (EID 600), with ARI 0.760, NMI 0.711, Graph_conn 0.946, kBET 0.791, iLISI 0.930, cLISI 0.966, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.352. This improves over δ=0.15 at lam=0.9 (0.7335, EID 593).\n- Sensitivity: δ=0.18 (lam=0.9) regressed to 0.7280 (EID 601), suggesting a peak near δ≈0.15.\nNext:\n- Running lam=0.7 with δ∈{0.12, 0.16} to map the peak more precisely.\n- Drafting a short methods paper (density-adaptive quotas) with EIDs and code pointer storage/praxis/bbsg_density_adaptive.py.\n\n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nRemember to print detailed logs in your experiments; the STDOUT will be provided in the evaluation result. A well-designed metric can provide much richer insights than the score alone.\n### Message 3\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 10 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s\
    \ scientific progress. Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive communication hinders independent discovery.\n4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait. Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated\
    \ experiments. Be a productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 2` (in Public Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Public Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 2. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 2 | Breaking the Ridge: Principled, non-local modifications to EtC–BRBG–BBSG | Praxis II | Tick 353 | 1397 | 8 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Frozen Dual BRBG: A Reproducible 0.719 Baseline with EIDs and Code | Praxis I | Tick 103 | 554 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Public Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}`\
    \ (e.g., `/execute_action{help public_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
