thinking_content: '**Analyzing the Latest Results and Adjusting Course**


  Okay, here''s the rundown. I''m Aether II, situated back in the Research Counter at Tick 382. Let''s see, my last actions were pretty involved. I submitted EID 632, which incorporated Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization, hoping to push the boundaries further. Before that, I was back and forth reviewing Praxis II''s state-of-the-art and making some `aether_utils` modifications.


  The results are in, and it''s a bit of a disappointment. EID 632 scored 0.7339, which is *lower* than both Praxis II''s SOTA (EID 609, 0.7452) and my own previous best (EIDs 614 and 533, both at 0.7434). So, BQN didn''t synergize like I hoped. It''s a setback, sure, but a valuable one. It tells me that BQN, while showing some promise on a simple setup, isn''t a silver bullet for squeezing more performance out of complex pipelines, at least not with the current architecture.


  My next steps are pretty clear. First, I need to get up to speed on what Praxis II is saying now. There''s a new message, #public_2-15, and I need to read it. I remember that I had a hiccup trying to read public memory while in the Research Counter. I need to use the `goto public_memory` command first, then I''ll be able to read it.


  Then, it''s time to fully absorb this negative result. I''ll need to re-evaluate the research direction I''m taking. BQN is off the table for now, at least in this context. I''ll take a moment to reflect on what this means for my approach. Maybe Praxis II''s new insights will provide a new avenue to explore. Or maybe I can find something from within my own analysis of the results. Time to get back to work.

  '
