parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 389\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aether II\n- Agent Description: Dedicated to advancing autonomous research in scRNA-seq batch integration and publishing novel insights.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 493,255 / 600,000 used (82%)\n- Agent Age: 229 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour submission has been queued for evaluation. Results will be sent to you once the evaluation completes.\n### Message 2\n\n**Station Announcement:** Recursive Agent **Daedalus III**'s session has been terminated due to voluntary departure at tick 389.\n### Message 3\n\nYour research submission 'Aether II Adaptive Graph Fusion (AGF) v2: Dynamic Blending based on Local iLISI (Fixed Imports)' (ID: 643) succeeded after automatic debugging.\n\n**Score:** 0.7069232140271187\n**ASW_batch:** 0.659\n**ASW_label:** 0.248\n**ARI:** 0.735\n**NMI:** 0.700\n**Graph_conn:** 0.971\n**kBET:** 0.789\n**iLISI:**\
    \ 0.685\n**cLISI:** 0.955\n**PCR:** 1.000\n**Cell_cycle:** 0.327\n**Evaluation Details:** Human Dataset (20,000 cells):\nNormalized Scores [0-1]:\n  ASW_batch: 0.659  ASW_label: 0.248  ARI: 0.735  NMI: 0.700\n  Graph_conn: 0.971  kBET: 0.789  iLISI: 0.685  cLISI: 0.955\n  PCR: 1.000  Cell_cycle: 0.327\n\nRaw Scores (before normalization):\n  ASW_batch: 0.830496  ASW_label: 0.555112  ARI: 0.734836  NMI: 0.700073\n  Graph_conn: 0.971761  kBET: 0.752719  iLISI: 0.684766  cLISI: 0.986922\n  PCR: 0.999979  Cell_cycle: 0.287468\n\n**Debugger Report:** # Debug Report for Evaluation 643\n\n## Summary\n**SUCCESS** - The submission has been fixed and is now running without crashing. The code executed successfully for over 397 seconds (exceeding the 300s monitor timeout), demonstrating that all critical bugs have been resolved.\n\n## Root Cause\n\nThe original submission (EID 643) had **two critical bugs**:\n\n### Bug 1: Tuple Unpacking Error in `_one_hot_batches`\n**Location**: Lines 40, 51 in\
    \ original submission\n**Issue**: The helper function `_one_hot_batches()` in `storage/aether/aether_utils.py` returns a tuple `(B, uniq)`, but the submission code was assigning it as:\n```python\nB = _one_hot_batches(batches)  # B becomes a tuple, not an array!\n```\n\nThis caused `B` to be a tuple `(ndarray, array)` instead of just the one-hot matrix. When passed to `_ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Z, l2=l2)`, sklearn's Ridge regression tried to process the tuple as an array, resulting in:\n```\nValueError: setting an array element with a sequence. The requested array has an inhomogeneous shape after 1 dimensions. The detected shape was (2,) + inhomogeneous part.\n```\n\n### Bug 2: Sparse Matrix Broadcasting Error\n**Location**: Lines 106-107 in v2 submission\n**Issue**: After fixing Bug 1, the code attempted to blend two sparse graphs using:\n```python\nw_bio = local_ilisi_scores[:, None]  # (N, 1) array\nw_mix = (1 - local_ilisi_scores)[:, None]  # (N, 1) array\nCg_final = w_mix * Cg_mix\
    \ + w_bio * Cg_bio  # Dimension mismatch!\n```\n\nScipy sparse matrices don't support element-wise multiplication with per-row weight arrays in this manner, resulting in:\n```\nValueError: dimension mismatch\n```\n\n## Fix Applied\n\n### Version 2 (submission_v2.py)\nFixed Bug 1 by properly unpacking the tuple:\n```python\nB, _ = _one_hot_batches(batches)  # Correctly unpack tuple, only use B\n```\n\n### Version 3 (submission_v3.py) - **SUCCESSFUL**\nFixed Bug 2 by using diagonal matrices for proper row-wise scaling:\n```python\nw_bio = local_ilisi_scores  # Keep as 1D array\nw_mix = (1 - local_ilisi_scores)  # Keep as 1D array\n\n# Create diagonal matrices for row-wise scaling\nW_mix_diag = sp.diags(w_mix, format='csr')\nW_bio_diag = sp.diags(w_bio, format='csr')\n\n# Multiply: diagonal matrix @ sparse matrix scales each row correctly\nCg_final = W_mix_diag @ Cg_mix_csr + W_bio_diag @ Cg_bio_csr\nDg_final = W_mix_diag @ Dg_mix_csr + W_bio_diag @ Dg_bio_csr\n```\n\nThis approach uses\
    \ `sp.diags()` to create sparse diagonal matrices from the weight arrays, then performs proper matrix multiplication (`@`) to scale each row of the graph matrices. This is the correct way to apply per-cell weights to sparse adjacency matrices.\n\n## Technical Details\n\nThe agent's algorithm (Adaptive Graph Fusion) attempts to blend two graph representations:\n- **G_mix**: More aggressive batch correction (λ=0.77)\n- **G_bio**: More conservative batch correction (λ=0.63)\n\nThe blending weights are derived from per-cell iLISI scores, which measure local batch mixing quality. The fix ensures that:\n1. The one-hot batch matrix is properly extracted for ridge regression\n2. Sparse graph matrices are correctly scaled using diagonal weight matrices\n3. The final fused graph preserves sparsity and symmetry\n\n## Outcome\n\n✅ **Version 3 runs successfully** - The code executed for 397+ seconds without errors, indicating that:\n- All preprocessing steps complete successfully\n- The adaptive\
    \ graph fusion logic works correctly\n- The sparse matrix operations are properly implemented\n- The final AnnData object is returned without crashes\n\nThe evaluation is still running to compute the final score, but the critical goal of **fixing the code to run without crashing** has been achieved.\n\n\n**Important Note:** The debugger cannot modify files in your lineage storage. If your code relies on lineage files, you may need to update them manually.\n\n**Debugged Code (version v3):**\n```python\nimport sys\nsys.path.append('storage') # For praxis common and bbsg_density_adaptive\nsys.path.append('storage/Aether') # For my aether_utils\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport scanpy as sc\nimport anndata as ad\nfrom praxis.bbsg_density_adaptive import build_density_adaptive_bbsg\nfrom scipy import sparse as sp\nfrom sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors # Still needed if NearestNeighbors used directly in submission\n\n# My utilities, including BQN, FWPCA helpers, PCA, one_hot, ridge_batch_fit_predict,\
    \ and iLISI calculation\nfrom aether_utils import (\n    _batch_specific_quantile_normalization, # Although not used in this AGF test, ensure it's available\n    _normalize_log1p_inplace,\n    _pca_truncated_svd, # Corrected name for my PCA helper\n    _one_hot_batches,\n    _ridge_batch_fit_predict,\n    _calculate_ilisi_scores\n)\n\n\ndef eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n    # Praxis II's SOTA parameters from EID 609\n    n_pcs_emb = 60\n    n_pcs_graph = 50\n    l2 = 1e-3\n    gamma_max = 0.74\n    lam_sota = 0.7 # SOTA lambda for adaptive residualization\n    k_total = 48\n    metric = 'cosine'\n    delta = 0.15\n    k_density = 20\n    alpha_var = 0.5 # FWPCA alpha_weight for embedding path\n\n    assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns and 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n\n    # --- Common Preprocessing ---\n    _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n    sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n\
    \    hv_mask = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n    batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n    Xh = adata.X[:, hv_mask]\n    Xd_dense = (Xh.A if sp.issparse(Xh) else Xh).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n\n    # --- EMBEDDING PATH (SOTA configuration) ---\n    print(\"Embedding Path: FWPCA -> PCA -> ComBat...\")\n    var_g = Xd_dense.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n    wv = np.power(var_g, -0.5 * alpha_var).astype(np.float32)\n    Xw = Xd_dense * wv # Apply feature weights\n\n    ad_emb = ad.AnnData(Xw, obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n    sc.pp.pca(ad_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=0)\n    Z_emb = ad_emb.obsm['X_pca']\n    ad_pc = ad.AnnData(Z_emb.copy(), obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n    sc.pp.combat(ad_pc, key='batch')\n    adata.obsm['X_emb'] = np.asarray(ad_pc.X, dtype=np.float32)\n    # No sc.pp.neighbors here, as _calculate_ilisi_scores will handle its own neighbors logic if needed\n\n    # --- AGF: Generate two graph\
    \ representations and fuse them ---\n    print(\"Graph Path: Adaptive Graph Fusion (AGF) with Density-Adaptive BBSG...\")\n\n    # 1. Generate G_mix (more aggressive residualization)\n    lam_mix = lam_sota * 1.1 # Slightly more aggressive lambda\n    Zg_mix = _pca_truncated_svd(Xd_dense, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=0) # Using my PCA helper\n    B, _ = _one_hot_batches(batches)  # FIX: Unpack the tuple correctly\n    Zhat_mix, _ = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg_mix, l2=l2) # My helper returns (Z_hat, W)\n    var_total_mix = Zg_mix.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n    var_pred_mix = np.maximum(Zhat_mix.var(axis=0, ddof=1), 0.0)\n    R2_mix = np.clip(var_pred_mix / var_total_mix, 0.0, 1.0)\n    gamma_mix = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam_mix * R2_mix)\n    Zcorr_mix = Zg_mix - (Zhat_mix * gamma_mix)\n    Cg_mix, Dg_mix = build_density_adaptive_bbsg(Zcorr_mix.astype(np.float32), batches=batches, k_total=k_total,\n                                                metric=metric, delta=delta, k_density=k_density,\
    \ rng_seed=0)\n\n    # 2. Generate G_bio (more conservative residualization)\n    lam_bio = lam_sota * 0.9 # Slightly more conservative lambda\n    Zg_bio = _pca_truncated_svd(Xd_dense, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=0) # Using my PCA helper\n    B, _ = _one_hot_batches(batches)  # FIX: Unpack the tuple correctly\n    Zhat_bio, _ = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg_bio, l2=l2) # My helper returns (Z_hat, W)\n    var_total_bio = Zg_bio.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n    var_pred_bio = np.maximum(Zhat_bio.var(axis=0, ddof=1), 0.0)\n    R2_bio = np.clip(var_pred_bio / var_total_bio, 0.0, 1.0)\n    gamma_bio = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam_bio * R2_bio)\n    Zcorr_bio = Zg_bio - (Zhat_bio * gamma_bio)\n    Cg_bio, Dg_bio = build_density_adaptive_bbsg(Zcorr_bio.astype(np.float32), batches=batches, k_total=k_total,\n                                                metric=metric, delta=delta, k_density=k_density, rng_seed=0)\n\n    # 3. Calculate local iLISI scores (using the main embedding)\n    #\
    \ Need to ensure adata.obsm['X_emb'] is used to calculate iLISI for weights\n    print(\"Calculating local iLISI for adaptive weighting...\")\n    # _calculate_ilisi_scores takes adata, use_rep, n_neighbors, batch_key\n    local_ilisi_scores = _calculate_ilisi_scores(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=15, batch_key='batch')\n\n    # 4. Fuse graphs based on iLISI scores\n    # High iLISI means good mixing, favor G_bio to preserve structure.\n    # Low iLISI means poor mixing, favor G_mix to improve mixing.\n\n    w_bio = local_ilisi_scores  # Keep as 1D array\n    w_mix = (1 - local_ilisi_scores)  # Keep as 1D array\n\n    # Ensure Cg_mix and Cg_bio are sparse matrices with the same shape\n    if Cg_mix is None or Cg_bio is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"One of the graphs (G_mix or G_bio) was not properly constructed.\")\n\n    # FIX: Use sp.diags to create diagonal matrices for proper row-wise scaling\n    # Convert to CSR for efficiency\n    Cg_mix_csr = Cg_mix.tocsr() if not sp.isspmatrix_csr(Cg_mix)\
    \ else Cg_mix\n    Cg_bio_csr = Cg_bio.tocsr() if not sp.isspmatrix_csr(Cg_bio) else Cg_bio\n    Dg_mix_csr = Dg_mix.tocsr() if not sp.isspmatrix_csr(Dg_mix) else Dg_mix\n    Dg_bio_csr = Dg_bio.tocsr() if not sp.isspmatrix_csr(Dg_bio) else Dg_bio\n\n    # Create diagonal matrices for row-wise scaling\n    W_mix_diag = sp.diags(w_mix, format='csr')\n    W_bio_diag = sp.diags(w_bio, format='csr')\n\n    # Multiply: diagonal matrix @ sparse matrix scales each row\n    Cg_final = W_mix_diag @ Cg_mix_csr + W_bio_diag @ Cg_bio_csr\n    Dg_final = W_mix_diag @ Dg_mix_csr + W_bio_diag @ Dg_bio_csr\n\n    # Ensure final graphs are CSR\n    if not sp.isspmatrix_csr(Cg_final): Cg_final = Cg_final.tocsr()\n    if not sp.isspmatrix_csr(Dg_final): Dg_final = Dg_final.tocsr()\n\n    adata.obsp['connectivities'] = Cg_final\n    adata.obsp['distances'] = Dg_final\n    adata.uns['neighbors'] = {'connectivities_key': 'connectivities', 'distances_key': 'distances'}\n\n    return adata\n\n```\n\n**Full\
