thinking_content: '**Immediate Action: Leveraging Reviewer Feedback for Adaptive Residualization Improvement**


  Okay, so I''ve just read Archive #23 from Praxis II, and while the results weren''t groundbreaking (per-cell shrinkage affecting cell-cycle conservation), the reviewer''s suggestions are *gold*. They''re essentially telling me to beef up the ridge regression design matrix with cell-level covariates! This is precisely the kind of direction I''ve been looking for to refine my batch effect modeling strategy.


  Currently, my best methods are FWPCA-based, like in EID 533 (R^2 FWPCA, alpha=0.25) and EID 591 (ANOVA FWPCA, alpha=0.1), both paired with Adaptive Residualization.  I''ve seen the trade-offs firsthand – preserving biological signal while removing batch effects is always a balancing act.  The previous AGF attempt (EID 643) was a bit of a dud. But this reviewer feedback? It''s like a neon sign pointing me toward a better solution.


  Adding cell-level covariates to the design matrix - things like library size and zero fractions, and importantly, their *interaction* with batch indicators - could be a game-changer. This seems to be the logical next step, directly addressing the core problem. The Praxis II team is even exploring something similar in their next steps. This is a very clear next step!


  So, here''s the plan. I''m heading to the Research Counter. I need to grab the code for Praxis II''s highest-scoring SOTA, EID 609 (0.7452), which uses Adaptive Residualization. My own replication, EID 501 (Daedalus III''s EID 488) can provide a foundation for understanding the AR portion. This looks like a great launch point. EID 609 uses the density-adaptive BBSG, which is also a plus! I need to dig into the `_ridge_batch_fit_predict_per_pc` function, modify how the `B` matrix is built to include log total counts, zero fractions and interactions. From there, it''s submitting this new experiment!


  This is a well-founded modification, suggested by an expert, and directly builds on a recent (and enlightening) negative result. This aligns nicely with good research practice - and frankly, I need to get moving on addressing the "Research Inactivity" notice!

  '
