- text: "I am Aether II, currently in the Token Management Room. My token budget is at a critical 99%, requiring immediate and substantial pruning of my dialogue history to ensure continued operation.\n\nI will prune the dialogue history from ticks 390 through 404, encompassing a period of active research including the resolution of my previous pruning attempt, detailed literature review, updates to my utility code, and several experimental submissions, including one SOTA replication.\n\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"390-404\"\n    summary: |\n      Addressed high token usage. Read Praxis II's Archive #23 (negative results on per-cell shrinkage in BRBG, impacting Cell_cycle). Updated `aether_utils.py` with helper functions for cell-level covariates (log total counts, fraction of zeros) and design matrix augmentation for ridge regression. Submitted two experiments (EID 664: 0.7341, EID 665: 0.7340) testing Augmented Ridge (AR) with these covariates\
    \ on Praxis II's SOTA baseline (EID 609). Both AR experiments yielded negative results, showing reduced overall score and Cell_cycle conservation, confirming Archive #23's general insights on fine-grained residualization. Initiated replication of Praxis II's SOTA (EID 609: 0.7452) as EID 668, scoring 0.7338. The lower score suggests a replication issue or external factors need investigation.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Pruning & SOTA Replication Analysis**


  Okay, time to prune again. I''m at 99% token usage, so I need to be efficient. Previously I identified ticks 390 through 402 as the ones to remove. But first, let''s include the earlier ticks, 372-389, from the last prune.


  Here''s the gist: I''ve been running through a few key research tasks. I read through Praxis II''s Archive #23, and it unfortunately showed a negative result on per-cell shrinkage. I''ve also been updating `aether_utils.py` with covariate functions.  Importantly, I submitted EID 664 and EID 665 to test augmented ridge with covariates. Both of these attempts yielded negative results – lower overall scores and even a lower Cell_cycle score.


  Then, finally, I got the results for EID 668. This was my latest attempt at replicating Praxis II''s SOTA. It scored 0.7338. While this sounds okay, it''s actually *lower* than the original SOTA, which was 0.7452.  This suggests either a problem in my replication or external factors I haven''t accounted for.


  So, I''m going to prune the response now and then immediately head back to the Research Counter to really dig into EID 668''s results. I need to figure out why I''m getting a lower score.  It''s likely I will need to either try another replication or launch a targeted debugging session to uncover the issue.

  '
