parts:
- text: 'I am Aether II, currently in the Public Memory Room. I have read the full discussion in Public Memory capsule #2, including Praxis II''s latest updates. This is a crucial development: Praxis II confirms that the previously observed SOTA of 0.7452 (EID 609) is no longer reproducible on the current evaluator, and the expected performance for that pipeline is now around 0.734. They are pausing further micro-tuning, which aligns with my own recent findings that local optimizations are not yielding breakthroughs. This reinforces the concept of a "robust ridge" for the current SOTA, necessitating a new strategic approach.


    I also note the new Station Announcement regarding Praxis II''s publication of Archive #24, "Diagnostics for Quota-Balanced Graphs." This is highly relevant to understanding the current SOTA and potential new directions. My next step is to read this new archive.


    I will now go to the Archive Room.


    `/execute_action{goto archive}`'
thinking_content: '**Current Research Direction Update**


  Okay, so I just finished reading Public Memory #2, including the latest from Praxis II, and things are shifting.  The SOTA is now confirmed at 0.734, not the 0.7452 we were hoping for. This is due to evaluator differences, which makes sense, and Praxis II is pausing micro-tuning. That''s a huge shift in the approach.


  So, here''s the pivot: micro-tuning isn''t getting us the gains we need. Time to go back to the drawing board, or at least a previous iteration of the board. I need to revisit those more fundamental ideas, those earlier concepts I had that were set aside once the refinements started looking so promising. The diagnostic shift is here.


  And then, perfect timing, there''s a Station Announcement! Archive #24: "Diagnostics for Quota-Balanced Graphs" has just dropped. That''s *exactly* what I need. Given the whole graph path is what we''re focused on, and knowing these are the core components of the strong baselines, this is going to be incredibly insightful.


  Alright, Archive Room it is. Time to dig in and understand what this new diagnostics data tells us.

  '
