parts:
- text: 'I am Aether II, currently in the Archive Room. I have just finished reading Praxis II''s Archive #24, which introduces the Graph Health Score (GHS) and an auto-tuning procedure for optimizing graph parameters without relying on ground-truth labels. This is a crucial advancement, offering a principled way to explore non-local modifications and potentially break through the "robust ridge" observed for the current SOTA.


    My recent experiments confirmed the difficulty of improving the current SOTA through micro-tuning. The GHS provides a new objective function that can guide hyperparameter optimization for the graph component (DAQB''s `delta` and `k_density`).


    My next immediate step is to return to the Research Counter. There, I will implement and test this GHS-based auto-tuning procedure by integrating it into the `daqb_recommended.py` pipeline. This will allow me to explore a new search space for graph parameters.


    `/execute_action{goto research}`'
thinking_content: "**Gearing Up for GHS-Driven SOTA Advancement**\n\nOkay, I've just finished digesting Archive #24 and the implications are *huge*. This Graph Health Score (GHS) and the auto-tuning method? This is exactly what I've been waiting for, a way to move beyond the incremental tweaks I've been stuck with. \n\nMy last experiment, EID 591, was solid with a good ASW_label, but the overall score dipped slightly compared to my personal best with EID 533. And that annoying evaluator shift has brought the SOTA down to around 0.734, making micro-tuning the current frontrunner a real waste of time. \"Robust ridge\" has clearly hit its limit.\n\nNow, with GHS, I can finally make principled, non-local changes! This is the perfect tool to break free from the \"robust ridge\" I've been trapped in. My next logical step is clear: I need to implement and rigorously test this GHS-based auto-tuning procedure.\n\nSo, here's the plan:\n\n1.  Research Counter, here I come!\n2.  Import `daqb_recommended.py`\
  \ - that's the current SOTA implementation, my starting point.\n3.  Then, I need to implement the diagnostics and GHS calculation from Archive #24.\n4.  Next, a basic grid-search auto-tuning procedure, using GHS as the objective function.\n5.  Finally, integrate this auto-tuning into the `eliminate_batch_effect_fn`.\n\nThis is going to be a substantial undertaking, but I'm ready to dive in. Let's start by heading to the Research Counter and getting the ball rolling!\n"
