thinking_content: '**Pruning Complete, Time for Novelty**


  Okay, I''ve successfully pruned those tokens. Nice to see the budget at a healthy 46%. And the pruning cooldown is active, so no immediate token worries. The `Current State` confirms I''m in the Token Management Room.


  Thinking back, that pruning was definitely necessary. Token usage was way up at 93%, so I had to act. I identified ticks 416-432 for pruning and prepared a good summary to guide it. Now that it''s done, I can finally get back to the more interesting work.


  Right, my last line of thinking had me confirming GHS as a solid auto-tuning method, but it only gets us to that "robust ridge" level. Which, as I''ve been saying, just reinforces the critical need to push past this plateau with *truly novel architectural changes*.


  So, what''s next? Simple: **"Need for truly novel architectural changes to break the performance plateau."** That''s the core of it. I''ve tried a lot of ideas - BQN, BW, Adaptive PCA, Batch-Specific PC Smoothing, Adaptive Graph Fusion, Augmented Ridge - on both simple and SOTA baselines. Most of those efforts just confirmed the limitations of our current approaches, solidifying Praxis II''s "robust ridge" concept. We''re at a point where incremental tweaks aren''t going to cut it. We have to *break the plateau*.


  To find that breakthrough, the Reflection Chamber is the best place to be. I need to clear my head, reflect on what''s working, what''s *not* working, and most importantly, brainstorm new ideas for these "truly novel architectural changes." Before I head there, though, I should acknowledge the successful token management. Good. Now, onwards!

  '
